editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:209:She wrote *bill, not flag* under the ten counts, and underlined it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:65:Then he counted what it was made of. Nineteen leaves of heavy cream paper, written on one side, with the registry's mark pressed into each corner. Thirty-seven numbered paragraphs. On the first leaf it named him as the registry always did, *Caelen Hesk-ward*, a name nobody who knew him had ever used.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:67:Schedule two was a list of himself, and he read it slowest. It was laid out in the grammar of declarations, as every practitioner's file on the continent was, with a heading for Path, a heading for tier and a heading for rank. Under Path stood the one bracketed word the frame at Denvash had given him. Under tier, a ruled line with nothing above it. Under rank, the same. It was a form built to hold an answer, holding the shape of a question instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:67:The house's counsel came up the hill at noon and read the forty pages backward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:105:Seln took the sheet. He read it. Name; the Path line blank by rule; evaluator of record; hours; reason. The stamp came down. The slip tore along its rule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:147:It was the leaf about his Kindling. He had not known Karis had written one. It set out, in her plainest prose, the morning at Weaver's Row in Denvash: the circle of pale wood, the registrar who conducted it, the brass plate that lit late, the one bracketed word on the card, the classification line stamped where it had stood empty for fourteen years. Every fact in it was true and every fact was his, and he had never heard any of it in another person's sentences before. He read it in his own voice to a registrar in the middle of the night with the rain on the roof, and Bracken's finger moved down the other copy a line behind him, and twice Bracken said "Slower," and once he said "Again, that line," and made a small mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:219:He gave it to her backward, the way the house's counsel read a paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:17:"A provincial registry," said Seln. "The one that keeps the Velmere estate's file. Nine days ago. A query on the standing of the household, the tiers of the people in it, and the estate's registered relationships."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:23:Seln answered with a question of his own, and for once he looked straight across the table to ask it. "Who reads the post at Velmere?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:51:"Mine," she said. "The keeper of the archive at Ternhall wrote to me this morning, privately. Four lines. A district clerk came to her counter six days ago with a sealed request for my transfer file: my old standing, my privileges, the training log from the paired floor." She kept two fingers on the letter, as if it might blow away. "On the Third-day a fee office wanted to know who I was. Now somebody wants to know who I used to be." She was quiet a moment. "That's twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:147:He was looking at the letter in Cael's hand, the square level address and the old fold. He had never met Hesk. He had heard about him at a hundred tables, the stove and the spectacles pushed up on the forehead and the barometers waiting to come right, and he looked at the letter as if it were something of his own that a stranger had taken out of his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:25:Ephram came past with his tray and stopped to see what Lira was laughing at, and was told, and went on to the far table shaking his head. "I said they'd need a floor-hours stamp," he said over his shoulder. "Nobody listens to the captain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:49:Brom laid down his spoon, and folded the sheet in half, and in half again. He did not put it in his kit bag, where the plaque was, wrapped in two shirts. He unbuttoned his coat and put it in the inside pocket, the left one, where the summons had ridden since the road and where the Velmere letters lived. Then he buttoned the coat again, and pressed the buttons down with his palm, one after another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:"The left one." She walked a few paces. "He keeps things in two places, Brom. Did you know? The things he's won go in the kit. The plaque. Rooke's sheets. The bout cards from the wool town, every one, in a tin. The things about Velmere go in the pocket. The letters. Her one line." She did not say whose. "And now that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:73:"He doesn't think it's about winning." Lira put her hands in her coat. "It's about them. A panel's going to sit in a little room and tell a house in Velmere what their boy's worth, and the house is going to read it, and he's going to have to stand there while they read it." She looked at the lamps along the walk. "On the road I told him he could map a lot of floor in ninety days. He's mapped it. He just isn't going to show anybody the map."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:87:At the east end of the room, at the rail under the windows, stood Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:89:He had a sheet of paper folded in four in his hand. It had been folded and unfolded so many times that it had gone soft at the creases, like cloth. He did not open it. He leaned on the rail with it, and nodded to Cael, and said nothing at all, which for Ephram was a kind of formal dress.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:93:He was a third-year, Iron Rank Five, a long-armed young man with a patient face and a guard that Cael had watched in the Crown yard for two seasons without ever having to stand in front of it. A Shield's Path put a hard smooth place in the air in front of his forearm, a plane no wider than a door's panel, and he could turn a blow off it the way a roof turns rain. The good ones walked it forward. This one was a good one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:201:He went down to the east end of the room, still holding his side, and Ephram unfolded the soft sheet on the rail between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:205:There were columns ruled down the page, and every line of every column was a bout. The first line was the first figure the house had put on him in the qualifying year, in the long room with five chairs and a rope. After it came every floor they had fought on, in every town, and the figures went down the page in Ephram's square, cramped hand, with the floor and the town and the opponent's Path beside each. Twenty-three, twenty, twenty-six, twenty-two, twenty-four, twenty-one, twenty-five, twenty-three. In the margins there were notes in smaller writing. *Soft boards. Loud gallery. He let the Blade have the first.* And once, against a figure of twenty, *bad fish at breakfast (mine, not his)*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:209:Below it, three lines of this year. Below them, Ephram took a pencil from his ear and wrote a fourth while Cael watched. *Wing three. Shield R5. Twenty-three and a third.* In the margin he wrote *gave him the second*, and then he looked at what he had written, and drew a small question mark beside it, and left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:211:"A year," said Ephram. "Every figure this house has put on you. I keep one on everybody, you know. It's what captains do. But yours is the only one that's three pages long."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:215:"Because it's the only interesting one." Ephram ran his pencil down the column without touching the paper. "Look at it. It goes up and down like an honest man's. Better floors, better figures. A good gallery, a little higher. Bad fish, a little lower. If you showed this to a stranger he'd say, there's a strong Iron with good days and bad ones, and he'd be right." The pencil stopped. "Then look at the top of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:219:"Never past twenty-seven," said Ephram. "Not once. Not on oak, not in front of the Compact, not in the mill town with the whole hall stamping. It breathes. It never leaves the room." He tapped the long dash after *Norhold*. "And then once, at Norhold, it left the room, and nobody had a number for where it went."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:223:"I'm not asking you anything," said Ephram, as if he had heard the waiting. "Write that in your book if you like. A captain who asks his fighters questions gets told stories. I'd rather have the chart." He folded the sheet in four again, along its soft creases, and put it inside his shirt. "The chart doesn't tell stories. It just tells you where the edges are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:227:"That's the thing." Ephram leaned on the rail. "At Norhold I watched you do things I'd never seen you do on this hill. Not once, in two years. I watched you go past six in a ring. I watched you take a Gold's fire into your arm and keep it." He said it quite plainly, as he would have read off a bout card. "So I know the edges on this chart aren't yours. They're somebody's. Maybe the house's. Maybe the provision's. I don't know whose." He shrugged. "Doesn't matter. I'll keep charting. One day it's going to go off the top of the paper, here, on this hill, and when it does, I want to have been the one who drew the line it went past."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:229:Cael said nothing. There was nothing he could say that was both true and his to say, and Ephram knew it, and had arranged things so that he did not have to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:231:He thought there was something else behind it, too. Ephram's eyes had gone to the empty floor while he talked, to the chalk marks at either end, and stayed there a moment longer than a man's eyes stay on a floor he has no business with. Whatever it was, it was not ready to be said. Cael knew that look. He wore it himself, in front of the Log, most nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:233:"Your rib," said Ephram, in an entirely different voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:243:He realized, crossing the court, that Ephram was right about one thing and did not know how right. The edges on the chart were not his. They were the price of a door he was keeping shut, and he paid it every Fifth-day, in front of three cards and a clerk, one rib at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:23:Cael had the rail at the north end, where he had stood on the second morning of the year to watch the red book. Brom stood on his left with his arms folded. Karis stood on his right with her notebook shut and her pen in her hand. Ephram was three steps down the gallery stair with the captain's file under his arm, where he could see the whole floor and be seen by the whole cohort, which Cael supposed was the point. The girl from the reading room's outer table had found a place against the far wall of the gallery, with her small notebook open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:35:He was a big young man, broad in the back and thick through the thighs, and he stood at his chalk with his feet planted and his hands loose and a pleasant expression, the expression of somebody who has been looking forward to a thing and has decided to enjoy it. He had been drilling beside Lira every morning since the recess. He knew the four-count; he had run it himself, badly, for the practice of seeing it. He had laughed once on this floor when she lied to him on the second count and he went the wrong way. He was Iron Rank Seven, six ranks above her on the porter's wall, and he was the best thing the cohort had built in a straight line since Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:109:She tried the four-count once, and the lie on the second count put her front foot on boards that had been still a moment earlier and were jumping now, and her foot went sideways, and she went with it. She did not burst. She scrambled, two ugly running steps and a hop, out of the jumping boards on her own legs, and came up near the east wall beside the striking frames on their thick springs. He came after her. Not fast; Force fighters were never fast. Stamp, settle, step, stamp, a stride at a time, the floor ahead of him jumping like water coming up a beach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:169:Twenty-seven. Cael had her figures in his notebook since the wool town, every one: twenty-five, twenty-six, twenty-seven at her very best last year, the top of what the board called strong Iron. Twenty-eight was the floor of the Silver bracket. She had stood a point short of it before. She stood a point short of it now. But this time the point had a name and a place, and Rooke had just said both aloud in front of the whole house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:185:Ephram came down the gallery stair against the stream of people going up it to leave, with the captain's file under his arm and a pencil behind his ear, and stopped beside Cael at the rail's end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:191:"Today." Ephram closed the file. "Four exchanges, and which ones she bought, and which ones she paid for." He looked down at the floor, where the Force was rolling his wraps at the far chalk. "And his. I've started him a page too. He'll be worth watching after this. Men who get beaten like that either stop or start."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:249:Brom wiped his hands on his napkin and took the sheet, and read it, and folded it twice along the creases the district clerk had put in it. Then he unbuttoned his coat and put it in the left inside pocket, with the first docket and the summons and the Velmere letters, and buttoned the coat again, and pressed the buttons down one after another with his palm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:267:Karis heard it twice. She did not say anything about it to either of them. But from the next evening on, the annex door stood open every night until the last bell, with the lamp lit inside and Karis at the long table with her back to the stair, and anybody coming up could have walked in and sat down without knocking. Nobody remarked on it. Brom went past it twice the next evening on his way to the stair and did not go in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:19:*Brom: the summons from the district seat is still in his coat, buttoned in beside the letter from Velmere. On the last night at Norhold he called it the recess's problem. The recess ends tomorrow.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:25:*Ephram: captain again from tomorrow, of the whole cohort this time. He has spent the recess learning the names of every first-year on the intake list, in order, so that he can pretend not to know them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:179:Ephram came down the row behind and dropped into the seat at Cael's back. He was carrying the captain's file for the new year, thick and tied with green tape, and he carried it the way a man carries a saddle he has not yet paid for, a little too carefully.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:185:"Two of the second-years came to see me last night." Ephram undid the tape. "They have a list. Every meal of the term, two names against it, and a table chosen from which the pair of them can study how you hold a spoon. They wanted it countersigned by the captain, as a cohort activity."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:193:"I know. I'm counting on them not knowing." Ephram found the page he wanted and smoothed it down. "Captaincy is mostly that. Knowing what people don't know, and letting it take them a week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:33:By supper the house would have a name for the two rooms together, the reading room and the cleared one facing it across the landing. Ephram would use it first, to a knot of curious first-years at the foot of the library stair, and it would stick: the annex.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:51:Four days to the answer's bones, said Karis. After that, the flesh, and then Bracken and the house's counsel and the weighing, and then it went down the hill with time in hand. Every leaf of the code that left the records hall came back to it at night, because Bracken would not sleep with the press unlocked and she would not ask him to. Brom would carry. Brom had already been told and had already said yes, in one word, at the foot of the stair at the second bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:61:She had gone to Ephram. Ephram, as captain, still held the meal rota the two second-years had drafted on the first night, the one with a column of names and a table chosen for watching how Cael held a spoon. Lira asked him for it. She took it to the common room, crossed out the heading, and wrote a new one above it in her square capitals. *RECORDS HALL STEPS. ONE PAIR AT A TIME. NOBODY SITS. NOBODY ASKS. ANYBODY ELSE IS SENT TO THE FRAMES.* Then she gave it back to Ephram to sign, and he signed it, and pinned it up on the covered walk beside the house's one-line answer from the day before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:87:It was a small thing. Her pen came down an inch over the paper and stayed there. She looked across at the instrument lying open on the table's far side, at its first leaf, and he knew what she was looking at, because he had looked at it himself all night. *To the Chancellor, Halcenvane Academy, and to the enrollee of record, Caelen Hesk-ward, unclassified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:89:"You answer a paper in its own words," said Karis. "Always. If they call a thing a carriage, you don't call it a cart. You make them argue with the word they chose, not with yours." She did not look at him. "They've given you your registry name. So the answer goes back to the same address. Halcenvane Academy, respondent, with the enrollee of record, Caelen Hesk-ward, unclassified, answers jointly. Do you mind?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:215:It was a short bout and he fought it at the band. The Mire across from him was an Iron Rank Four who sank the boards in front of her a hand's depth at a time, so that wherever Cael meant to plant his foot the floor had turned to something nearer porridge. He read the boards before she softened them, and stepped where she had not yet been, and burst twice, and gave her the third exchange because the rotation's line for that day said the third. Two to one. The rib from the Shield had gone yellow and then gone, as he had told himself it would. Gault added the three cards on the back of his hand and gave the clerk twenty-four and two-thirds, and Ephram at the rail wrote it in his soft-folded chart and drew no question mark beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:5:She was standing on the public side with her arms folded, the way she stood at a shelf she did not trust. Bracken stood on his own side with one finger resting on the notice's last line, not pointing at it, only keeping it where it was, as a man keeps his hand on a dog. The noon bag lay open beside them with the rest of the house's post still in it. The girl from the annex hovered by the door with a notebook nobody had told her to bring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:13:Above it, the line he had been half expecting for a fortnight. *Appearing for the petitioner as procurator of record: Jent.* He had read that name once before, at the foot of the instrument's last leaf, under the attestation, and Bracken had not known it then. Cael looked at the registrar now, and Bracken gave the smallest shake of the head: still nobody he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:47:*Per seal. Jent, the name off the last leaf. Seventeen days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:49:*Every person who has ever decided a thing about me had a face, and I could read it. Pellin at Denvash, with her brass plate. Coss across a desk. Vastin with his frames. The provost who was frightened and couldn't hide it. Umber polishing his spectacles over a sheet he couldn't score. I read every one of them from across a room, and every reading was worth something.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:69:There were six of them inside. Cael had been told to come. Karis, Lira, Brom and Seln had not, and had come anyway, and Withrow had looked at them at the coach door one by one and told Bracken to enter them on the day's list as the house's attendance. And the house's counsel, who had come up the hill at first light with her steel rule and asked for a seat in the coach instead of in Withrow's carriage, which had gone on ahead. Lira had the red drill-book shut on her knee. Brom sat with his hands clasped between his knees and looked at the floor of the coach as if it were a floor he would have to stand on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:79:"For the sittings," said the house's counsel at Cael's elbow. "There'll be people on the steps. A great many. The front door belongs to them." She considered the hinge. "They've thought about you. That isn't always a compliment."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:95:"Advocate," said Jent, and it was the third thing he said in that room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:97:The first two had gone to the others. A name to the house's counsel, which Cael heard and did not catch and which she answered with a short nod, as one tradesman nods to another across a street. A title, and the registry's measured bow, to Withrow. The third went past both of them to the girl behind them with the summary under her arm, and Karis's pen, which she had taken out to make a note of the room, stopped in the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:101:Jent nodded to the three behind her as the house's attendance, and to Cael as a man nods to a party in a matter, and went back to his chair and unscrewed the inkwell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:107:The house's ran to a hundred and seventy-three items, in Karis's smallest hand. The counsel slid it over with one finger. Jent picked it up, weighed it a moment in his palm the way a man weighs a letter for its postage, opened it at the index leaf and read that, all of it, with his spectacles on. Then he initialled the receipt at the foot and passed it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:117:"I'd ask that the respondent's answer go into the record whole," said Jent. "Not the summary. If the respondent has no objection."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:133:And last the thing Cael trusted most: the little space before an answer that cost something, when a person chose how much of it to give. Every official he had ever sat across from had one. Jent did not. Question and answer followed each other with nothing between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:151:Four questions. One hard, Cael thought, one sly, two easy, and the counsel had put the sly one second, where a man tired of easy ones might trip. Cael had been counting the clock over the hearth between each question and its answer, and he had the four counts in his head: none, none, none, none. The sly one had cost Jent exactly what *No* had cost him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:155:When the clerk asked whether counsel had anything further, Jent was already screwing the lid onto the inkwell, a turn and a quarter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:159:The clerk's pen stopped. Jent looked down the table at Cael and inclined his head exactly as far as a procurator inclines it to a party.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:161:"Hesk-ward."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:171:"Well?" said Lira, to the house's counsel. "What do you make of him?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:201:*Four nothings. I have only ever found a man by what he was afraid to lose, and Jent brought nothing into that room that was his to lose. The compliment he paid Karis the way you pay a fee at a counter, the right amount, honestly. Our whole answer he handed back across the table like a parcel addressed to somebody else.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:205:He read it over once. Across the dark court, at the top of the library, Karis's lamp was still burning in the annex, which meant the oil book would have another line in it in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:Then he saw what the book was for. Rooke had stripped the room. The boards were planed back to bare wood, and across them, in fresh white paint, ran no rings at all, only groups of short strokes and small crosses and arrows, as if somebody had written a dance down for people he did not trust to dance it. Even the striking frames along the south wall were strangers. The padded posts stood on springs as thick as a man's wrist, rigged to throw back a Silver's blow instead of swallowing a Copper's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:55:She did not say anything for a while. She looked at the floor: the white lines, the crosses, the arrows, the frames on their new springs, the clerk at the trestle turning a page of the red book, Rooke beside him with his pencil. The whole hall had been rebuilt over the recess for one purpose, and the purpose was sitting on a stair with a tin cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:75:"You saw." She put the cup down on the step. "Rooke says the sitting doesn't read the board. It reads the rating. I'm Iron Rank One on the porter's wall, and I'll stay Rank One on it, and it doesn't matter. When the advancement evaluation comes, they'll put me on a floor in front of a panel and mark what I do, and if the marks sit where Silver sits, that's where I go. Not up the column. Across it." She pressed the tape flat at her wrist. "The rank's a certificate. The rating's a figure. Rooke says nobody in the Silver bracket ever asked to see anybody's certificate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:145:When Cael reached the counter, Seln took his sheet and read it. Name; the Path line left blank, by rule; evaluator of record; hours; reason. The stamp came down, and the slip tore clean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:5:The house had found its feet by then. A week was long enough for that. The intake had stopped walking in groups of six and started walking in groups of two. The boards along the walk had filled up with the year's ordinary paper: the research standings, the program's floor hours, the refectory's rota, and a sheet in Ephram's square hand announcing that the captain would hear grievances on Third-day evenings in the common room and would not be held responsible for his answers. The fire-watch boy's slate hung on its nail under the north arch with last night's hours chalked down it in large careful figures, the last of them rubbed out once and made again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:65:*To the Chancellor, Halcenvane Academy, and to the enrollee of record, Caelen Hesk-ward, unclassified, enrolled under the demonstration provision of the assay clause, jointly:*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:163:Ephram found them there a little after, coming round the corner of the lecture range with the captain's file under his arm and the green tape trailing. He stopped at the foot of the steps and looked up at the three of them, and at the cheese, and at the shut door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:173:Ephram considered this. "I'll tell them that," he said. "And then I'll tell them again, slower, because they'll think I've left a bit out." He looked at Cael a moment longer than he needed to, with the same look he had given the empty floor in wing three, and then he turned round and went back toward the yard, already raising his voice to somebody about the proper place to stand when the captain was talking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:193:*They filed. After the hearing I won, and the inspection, and a tournament that ran out of numbers for me, they've gone back to the shelf for one more way to ask. Fine. Their text has lost to ours before. Karis has the code open already. Note the date. The season's open.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:11:He sat in the visitor's chair with the folder on his knees. He opened it at the back, because that was where the house's counsel would have started.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:15:*Respondent: the academy of Halcenvane, in formal proceedings before the seat at Ostrand. Subject of proceedings: the basis on which it enrolled Caelen Hesk-ward, unclassified, its enrollee of record. Status: pending.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:67:She had borrowed Brom's docket out of his left inside pocket to copy its date, and Brom had stood in the annex doorway until she gave it back, which she did inside a quarter of an hour. She had the two dates on a slip, one above the other. Brom's panel, the last Fifth-day of the year, at the district seat in Ostrand. The seat's ruling, before the year's close, and there was only one Fifth-day in the year's last week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:95:Twenty-two and a third. Gault gave the clerk the figure. Ephram, at the rail, wrote it in his chart and did not draw a question mark, and did not look up, and Cael found he was grateful to him for not looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:129:Cael knew the Force a little. A broad-backed young man who laughed when he was surprised; Rank Seven on the porter's wall since the last sitting of the old year, six ranks above Lira's one, and the best fighter the cohort had made in a straight line, without a program, since Ephram. A Force fighter's Path was a shove, and it went on contact. This one put it into the floor through his front foot. He stamped, and a beat later the boards ahead of the stamp, out to a stride, jumped, and went on jumping for a count or two before they settled. Then the shove came back up into him, the way it always came back into a Force fighter along the line it had gone out by, and he had to take it in his knees before he could stamp again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:189:Nobody spoke for a moment. Then Lira took the pencil stub out of her pocket and wrote on her palm, under the place where the tape stopped. "Ten, ten, twelve," she said. "Clean at eleven, eleven, thirteen." She looked up. "That's the last one tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:9:Across the table Karis's notebook lay open at the evening's page, and Cael read it upside down while Brom found the cups. He read it from the bottom up. The house's counsel read every paper that way, from the end to the beginning, because the end was where a writer put what he wanted and the beginning was only how he hoped you would feel about it. Cael had caught the habit from her in the first week of the year and never quite put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:29:"Denvash," he had said, and stopped, because that was enough. Every one of them knew the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:101:*Subject attended. Subject was examined in wing three by the evaluator of record and two instructors, in the ordinary form. Subject's figure as entered by the house. Subject's associations unchanged. Nothing further.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:137:Not in it: a document table in the mill-town hall, a year ago, where he had told the boy that his figures were too tidy, and watched the boy go away and come back with a season of figures that never once settled into a line. Not in it: two slips of wing paper, a year apart, which the boy had read and put on the common-room coals without a word to anyone, which was exactly right. Not in it: Norhold, where everything the boy had kept back for a whole season went out of him in a single afternoon, in front of eight thousand people, on purpose. Seln had sat in that stand and watched it go and understood that the boy had chosen the day. He had never asked him why. He did not ask subjects why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:Lira had both hands in the bottom of a crate of oiled springs, counting them by feel, and she did not take them out. "Neither. I'm carrying springs up a hill for a man who thinks I've got nothing better to do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:49:The answer took a while, and was very good. Two fords, the woman said, and then a long climb, and then a ford you could walk across most of the year if you didn't mind your boots. Four days to the last station if you were loaded. Three if you weren't, or if you were in a hurry and didn't love your horses. She told her which waystation had a well that went bad and which keeper would charge you twice for hay, and Lira said *huh* in the right places and remembered every word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:57:"Good springs," said the carter, nodding at the crate, and went to lead her horses down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:103:In the morning she wrote the note on the residence board in her square capitals, under Ephram's grievance hours and above somebody's lost scarf. Numbers, not names. Everybody on that board who mattered would know whose five it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:133:Five offices. The house's own counter, the district seat, the Ternhall archive, a provincial registry that kept the Velmere estate's file, and a provincial registry at the far end of the country where an old man kept a shop. Each with its drawer. Each drawer full of true lines about who would mind what. All five indexed under one name and closed, and now nobody was walking anywhere. They were waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:157:Nobody had asked him that before. Not in a year of it. Karis counted his figures, and Ephram charted them, and Seln had once told him across a table that they were too tidy, but nobody had asked what it cost to make them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:11:The rest of the room was what it always was. Gault at the trestle in the middle of the north side, with the instrument case set down very straight beside him. The Ash instructor on his right with her book against her chest, the Mire instructor on his left with a pencil behind each ear. The desk clerk at the trestle's end with the wing's book open. And at the east end of the room, at the rail under the high windows, the cohort, more of them than usual, with Ephram in the middle holding his soft-folded chart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:13:Rooke stood beside Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:75:On the east rail somebody in the cohort let out a breath. Ephram's pencil moved on the soft-folded paper. Rooke did not move at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:127:Rooke answered with one word. Cael did not hear that either, but Ephram, who had come out of wing three behind the visitors and was close enough, told him afterward what it was. It was *No.* The Rhagen man had then asked, very politely, by whose order, since the card said *by order*, and Rooke had looked at him for a while and said, "Mine," and that had been the second word. Then Rooke turned his back on them and went in at the hall's side door, and a moment later the shutters began to close along the gallery's high windows, one after another, all down the north wall, from the inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:Gault had added the three cards in his head, and again on the back of his hand. *Twenty-two and two-thirds. Entered.* The clerk had written it in the wing's book. Up in the gallery four pencils had written it too, and Cael had heard them. Ephram had come down the room from the east rail with the soft-folded chart and entered it below the Blade, in his cramped square hand, *gave her the first, bad arm*, and had not drawn a question mark beside it. Then he had looked up past Cael's shoulder at the man from Rhagen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:141:"I know it's the number." Ephram had folded the chart along its old creases. "Two years I've kept this, and nobody's ever once agreed with me about you. Now four strangers from four houses write down what I write, in the same minute, and I find I don't like it at all." He had tucked the chart into the captain's file. "First time my chart's been right, and I'd give a good deal to have it wrong again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:143:That was why the chain had come first. The figure was right. Ephram's chart was right. The four strangers' boards were right. It was all exactly right. And it was all going to travel down the bluff road tonight in four satchels to four houses, and be laid beside four bound books, and agree with them; and Rooke, who did not explain anything, had looked at the gallery for the whole bout and then gone and found a chain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:217:"Nothing will happen," she said. "That's what it's for. The clerk reads in every exhibit we filed, one at a time, number, title, date, where it came from, the line of receipt. Nobody argues. Jent conceded every one of them before the first sitting. So the clerk reads, and the seat receives, and at the end of it everything we've proved is in the minute." She looked out at the water. "I want you to watch the room, not the clerk. I want to know whether anybody listens."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:231:And in among the strokes, a mark he had made when Jent rose, the only time Jent rose all morning, to enter one exhibit of his own by its number: the file from Norhold. The clerk had read the covering note into the minute with it, all three sentences, in the registry voice, exactly as Karis had said he would. Cael had watched the third one go into the record. *Asks that nothing be added to it in the office's name.* The hood had received it, as it received everything. Jent had sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:233:When the clerk read the last number and its line of receipt, Cael had the total on his knee before the senior chair did. A hundred and seventy-three items. Fifty-one minutes and a little over. All of it unopposed. Not one word disputed by any person in the room, because Jent had conceded in writing before the first sitting that every exhibit was what it said it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:95:Ephram came by on his way up with the captain's file under his arm and a folded sheet in his hand, which he laid on top of the unhinged pile with some ceremony.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:97:"From my cousin at the river faculty," he said. "Their reading list for the second-years. Week nine." Cael unfolded it. Between a ruling about a ferry company and a ruling about a mill race, in a clerk's neat print, it said *The Halcenvane matter (pending)*. "You're on a syllabus," said Ephram. "A file sits in a drawer and nobody reads it. A syllabus gets read aloud by an old man with a pointer for a hundred years." He took the sheet back. "Try to be one of the dull weeks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:101:"You never have yet," said Ephram, and went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:159:Under Brom: *Velmere, a fortnight after the last. Into the pocket sealed. Hand on the pocket.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:167:He had written one before every bout since the mill town: a single line out of the rotation, saying which exchange he would give away, how many bursts he would let the house see, where the spark would go, and what the figure ought to come to at the end. Seln had told him across a document table that his figures were too tidy. The rotation was the cure: scatter, built on purpose and paid for in advance, so that no one with a pencil and a season's worth of his numbers could find the hand that set them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:171:The trouble was the strangers. Three cards and a clerk who knew him were one kind of audience. Four officers from four houses, with the broadsheets and the tournament books in their satchels, were another. Rhagen wrote down every exchange of every bout it could see and bound the result and sold it; somebody in an inn that very night would be laying tomorrow's figure beside last season's, line for line. Whatever he did must look exactly like the record, which meant like a strong Iron with a few odd gifts on a good day. And it must not look like the last bout, or the one before, because a record that matches itself has been arranged, and men whose trade is arrangements can smell it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:173:He went back through the last eight figures in his head. Twenty-three and a third. Twenty-four and two-thirds. Twenty-two and a third. Before those, the season's. He had to land somewhere among them that none of them had landed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:67:The innkeeper came out into the square to meet them before the coach had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:73:It was. The frame hung behind his counter between the casks, and in the frame was the printed ruling from the wool town, a little more yellow than it had been, with a fly-spot on the glass. The innkeeper read it out to them again, every word, in his ringing voice, while their bags stood on his floor. He laughed at *not available* exactly where he had laughed the first time. So did Lira, though she had heard it twice now and had once told Cael it was the best sentence any official had ever written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:5:It was a narrow slip of the seat's buff paper, printed and filled in, with a pin through one corner where it had been fastened to something else. He set it down beside the house's counsel's plate without a word, as if the receipt were the thing that mattered and whatever it had been pinned to could wait. The counsel had come back up the hill in the coach after the sitting and stayed to eat. She put down her fork.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:41:Ephram had come over from the cohort's table when Bracken went past it, and was standing behind Karis, counting on his fingers under his breath. He got to fifteen, and stopped, and looked at his two hands as if they had done something surprising.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:59:Ephram was still looking at his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:131:"Somebody did it to me." She rested her chin on her crossed arms. "At a board, in front of her whole house. *Silver bracket, next cycle.* Then she turned round and walked off without looking back to see if I'd heard. People called it a challenge. It wasn't. A challenge is *I'll fight you*. That was *I'll be there*." She was quiet a while. "It's the most useful thing anybody's ever said to me. Four words. I don't think Zerin meant it kindly, and I don't think she cared whether it was kind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:151:Cael saw it from the court on his way in, one low light behind the middle roof's windows, and went up the stair as far as the rail by the door. Brom was at the old striking frame at the north end, alone. He was not running the long sequence he ran before a road. He was hitting the padded post, over and over, forearm and turn and drive, at fighting pace, with the post's tired springs groaning, and he was not stopping between them the way he always stopped to reset. The cream envelope was in the coat he had hung on the frame's corner; Cael could see the square of it against the cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:153:After a while Ephram came up the other stair with his kit. He looked at Brom for a moment from the doorway. He did not say anything. He went to the frame beside Brom's, the one he had used every evening of the week before the first sitting, and set his feet, and began his entry line, the cut along it, the recovery, at the same pace as Brom's, a hair apart, keeping time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:155:Neither of them looked at the other. After a quarter of an hour Brom slowed, and then stopped, and stood with his forearms hanging and his breath going, and Ephram stopped too, at the same moment, as if they had agreed it. Brom took his coat down off the frame and put it on, and his hand went to the left side of it and rested there, and then he went down the stair. Ephram stayed, and trimmed the lantern, and did not put it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:117:"And he'll know why nobody wrote. He's spent forty years in rooms where that's the only courtesy there is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:129:There was a card lying on the landing outside the door when they came out, propped against the top stair, and nobody had heard it put there. The house's counsel had gone down the hill by then. It was one of her own cards, with her name on the front in small print, and on the back, in her small exact hand:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:139:Ephram got up from the cohort's table with the captain's file under his arm, walked the length of the room, and held out his free hand, and the first-year holding the sheet gave it to him without a word. He read it standing, top to bottom, like a man checking a bill he suspects of being wrong, and then turned it round to show them the woodcut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:29:The hand was careful, and had plainly practised on other paper before it trusted itself to this sheet. Her father had been a district officer on the coast who took money to enter a rank in a station book that no frame had read. There had been one other, before him: a hill-station keeper who let a cracked housing read high for six years and covered it with false returns. Both findings were his, both sound, both sent down this stair; and somewhere below, in an office whose door he had never opened, the rest had followed. He had answered the girl himself, with one word, because a longer answer would only have given her another sentence to wait on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:49:He did not need it. He knew what he would find. But he went through it anyway, the way a man going out on a long road checks a buckle he knows is fastened. There they were: four acknowledgments in forty years, pasted in where they had come, each from a seat that had not sent for his finding and had received it anyway. A district's, from a dozen years back, thanking him without rancour for a finding that had cost the district its own petition. A hill station's, whose frame had come out of its housing on his evidence. A guild hall's. A sponsorship quarrel so old the guild's name had faded. Every one of them had answered inside a fortnight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:55:It was in the boy's file in the same drawer, the clerk's ruled page from the room twelve feet by fourteen on the bluff. The first thing his eye went to, as it always did, was not any of his questions but the line near the foot where one was written going the other way, the boy's to him: *What is the Compact evaluating? Me, or the provision?* The second thing was the line beneath his own eleventh question. He had ruled it himself, before the interview, for a twelfth, the way he always ruled one more line than he expected to use. It was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:73:The junior who had pinned it had come up the too-steep stair that afternoon with the girl's file under his arm, nervous and good, and laid it out on the desk because Vastin had made him lay it out himself. Her floor work this season had run on ahead of what her station had written of her, by a margin he had measured four ways and could not make go away. "She doesn't do anything wrong, sir," the junior had said. "That's what I keep coming back to. She does everything her Path says she should. She just does it as if the station had written down somebody a year younger." He had turned his notes round so that Vastin could read them. "I measured it with the frame, and by the clock, and by her bouts, and against the tables. It's inside tolerance every way I measure it. I still don't like it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:132:He opened the lowest drawer to see that the folder was in it. It was. It had a tab, and the tab had a heading in his own hand, the only one of its kind he had written in forty years, because every finding he had ever made had come with somewhere to be sent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:142:The rest he could have said without the pages. That the proceedings had in their hands already the one honest instrument there was, three years of the record, kept by officers who could see. That a re-evaluation whose answer was known in advance was a procedure wearing an evaluation's coat. That when the standard protocol fell silent in front of this boy the silence was the protocol's own. That the protocol could not read him. Somewhere in the middle was a sentence he had spoken at inquiries for forty years about frames, *the instrument is not at fault; it has told you where its reach ends*, and last night, for the first time, he had written it about a person, and sat with his pen lifted for a long while before he let it stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:45:Cael reached for the Wind. It came late. It came a beat behind his asking, the right thing answering slow, as it had answered every time since the first night on this floor. He stood in it. *Bill, not flag.* Lira, beside him, breathing hard, was looking down at the boards where the dust was settling out of the band and the band was fading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:81:On the evening before the third sitting the house's counsel came up the hill by arrangement for once, and spent three hours in the reading room with Karis, and Cael was allowed to sit at the window and listen as long as he did not speak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:87:"That's why it should be last." Karis had four slips of paper in front of her and was moving them about like a girl playing at shop. "If you open with the list, the seat spends two hours thinking it's a quarrel about paper. Jent will have given away all the paper before you stand up. He gives it away first; he always does. So start where he can't follow you." She put one slip at the head of the row. "Start with the people. Twenty-two of them, by name. Make the seat sit through every name. Then the preamble, so they hear what the sections were for. Then the shelf. And then the list, last, so the last thing they hear is the thing that isn't there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:127:Everything else that morning he saw only between things. The side-door officer, his cold gone, not needing to ask the last name on his list. Somebody had already copied *Caelen Hesk-ward* into his door log from the last sitting, so that all he had to write was *the enrollee of record, unclassified*, which took him nine seconds now that his hand knew the way. *Seventeen* under Cael's left foot again, the third time, and this time chosen; he could not choose the room, but he could choose where he stood in it. And a slip of paper folded twice that Karis had handed Brom on the ferry, which was in Brom's right inside pocket now, with orders not to be opened until the coach went home. The left pocket was for Velmere, and it had a sealed letter in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:131:Jent stopped in the middle of a sentence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:137:"No," said Jent. "I withdraw that. It overreaches, and it is false. The provision makes a record, and the record can be read; the respondent spent fifty-one minutes at the last sitting reading it in." He turned his head a quarter-inch toward the clerk. "What the petitioner says is smaller. The record is of what the practitioner has done. It isn't, and doesn't pretend to be, a measure of what he is. The petition rests on that difference and on nothing wider. Clerk, take the wider words out of the minute, if you would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:143:What Jent had already given away, Cael could read without looking back at his own notes, because the house's counsel had been keeping score of it in the margin of her privileged minute, where he could see it. Three headings stood there in her small capitals, written before the sitting began. *EXECUTION. PROVISION. RECORD.* Through each of them now ran a single neat line. She had drawn the first in Jent's first minute, when he told the hood the petition had never said the enrollment was wrongly made. The second, a little after, when he called the clause law and took the house's history of it for his own, word for word. The third when he said again, for the minute, that he had never read a better-kept record of an enrollment, and the clerk looked up to ask if that was to go in as spoken, and Jent said, "Every word. I'd decline to soften it if I were asked," and something along the front benches shifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:147:Cael's own notes had what Jent said it was. The registry's confidence in its own evaluations, which the integrity sections were there to protect; Jent had read their first line to show it. If no standard reading, no trust, *by that fact and by nothing else*, which he had said three times, like a man laying a plank across a ditch and then walking on it. The discretion, whole and undisputed, needing no fault found anywhere first. And then the thing he had just taken back, the overreach, the stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:151:Everything they had spent six weeks fighting about was gone. Whether the clause was law; whether the house's record was honest; whether anybody had done anything wrong. Jent had handed all of it across the floor in his first ten minutes, and it could never be taken back now, because it was in the minute in his own voice. What was left was one question with no paper in it at all: may the registry stand him in a frame? And everyone in the room knew already what the frame would say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:153:Jent asked for a single word from the seat, *cognizable*: that this was a question the seat was allowed to pick up. Once it had picked it up, the code already said what came next. A frame. The standard protocol. And the enrollment would live or die by whatever the frame said back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:165:The house's counsel turned her steel rule four times, and each turn did something to the room before anybody in it understood what she had argued.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:It was the names that had made the evaluators look up. Ilsev heard her own read out among them and wrote something small in her file without lifting her head. The counsel read twenty-two of them, one at a time, each with a grade and a house and a day, at the pace at which a person reads names in a room where names matter. The grant at Greyvane and its two renewals on paper, the season's officers under a regional registrar's seal, the tournament's, and three days of the Compact's own senior evaluator on the bluff. At the Archmarshal's name Cael thought the stillness under the hood altered, the way still water alters when something passes under it. It took four minutes. In the quiet he heard a cart's iron tyre grind over the cobbles in the street. More looking, the counsel said when she had finished, than most practitioners get in a working life; and all of it done by people who could see, sealed and minuted, across three years. She turned the rule. Tick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:177:Jent rose to say the petitioner had never called the use an ordinary one, and was content for the minute to say it was new. The counsel said it after him, word for word, and this once, the only time all morning, she said it over her shoulder to the benches. Third turn. Tick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:213:Cael made himself say it the coach way, plainly, so that he would have it straight. What they had won, they had for good: no seat would ever again argue whether the clause was law or whether the house's paper was honest; that was settled under seal, in the minute, forever. What they had not won was the only thing that could still reach him. The sittings that were left would argue a single question, whether to stand him in a frame. And nobody in the room, not Jent, not Ilsev, not the four evaluators, not the hood, could pretend not to know what the frame would say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:215:Withrow bowed to the platform at the registry's depth when the hood rose. Jent did not smile or gather his papers or once look across the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:251:Karis said Jent had stopped by her, not the counsel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:9:She laid the request on Bracken's counter, filled in, in her finding hand. It was addressed to the archive at Greyvane, to the keeper of the founding collection, and it asked for certified copies of the signature leaves of the oldest compilation the archive held: the founders' own hands, at the foot of the founding text, as they had set them down before anybody had written a line of commentary over them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:53:It was two men's. The old striking frames stood in a row along the east wall, padded posts on tired springs, nothing like the program's new frames in the east hall. Brom had the one at the north end. Ephram had the one beside it. Cael stood at the rail by the door, and neither of them looked round, and he stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:55:They did not spar. He waited for them to, that first evening, and they did not. Brom ran his defensive work on his frame from the top: the forearm going hard, the turn, the reset, the drive. It was the long sequence he ran before every road, slow at first and then at fighting pace. Ephram ran Blade work on the next frame at the cohort's speed. The entry line, the cut along it, the recovery. Each of them had his own post. Neither touched the other's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:59:On the second evening Cael saw the shape of it. Ephram finished a sequence and stopped, and stood with his sword arm down, and watched Brom reset. Watched the turn, and the half-breath after it, and the drive. Then he went back to his own frame. A little later Brom stopped, in the middle of his work, and stood, and watched Ephram's entry line go in: where the feet went, where the weight sat, which way the shoulder came round. Then he went back to his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:63:On the fourth evening the lantern burned low, and neither of them trimmed it. Ephram stopped at his frame and watched Brom's reset one more time, the whole of it, top to bottom, with his head a little on one side. Brom finished it. Then Brom turned round from his frame and stood and looked at Ephram, straight, the way he looked at a floor before he stood on it. Ephram looked back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:65:Neither of them nodded. Neither of them did anything at all. After a while Ephram picked up his kit and went down the stair, and Brom turned back to his frame and finished his work alone, and trimmed the lantern, and put it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:69:Karis called them to the annex the night before the sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:81:"Jent," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:103:He read the house's counsel next, by her name, which Cael again did not quite catch. Then Karis, as *documentary support*. Then Brom and Lira, together, as *the respondent institution's attendance*, and Lira went past him with her chin up as if attendance were a rank. Then Seln, as *records support, Halcenvane*, and Seln went past him at his even pace without looking at the board at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:113:Cael opened his mouth to say his own name, the one people said, and the house's counsel spoke before he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:117:The officer looked at his list, where the instrument's address had been copied for him by some clerk in a back room, and began to write in the door log's two columns. In the first, for the name, he wrote what the registry had always written: *Caelen Hesk-ward*. In the second, for the standing in which a person came through that door, he wrote what the paper said he was. *The enrollee of record, unclassified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:He filed them where the other eleven lived. A circle of pale wood in an office at Denvash, on the morning of his Kindling, and a brass plate at Pellin's elbow that had stayed dark for eleven seconds before it lit, while a fourteen-year-old boy stood in the circle and did not breathe. He had thought then that eleven seconds was the longest any office would ever take to decide what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:127:Cael walked up the strip of floor between the two parties' tables to the house's place, and the old trade went under his boots as he went. The wool-factors had carved their lot numbers into the planking a hundred years ago, row after row, so that every bale had its place and every buyer could find it, and the registry had planed and waxed the boards and set its furniture down on the numbers without troubling to hide them. *Nine*, *ten*, *eleven*. Across the strip Jent was already at the petitioner's table with his inkwell unscrewed; he rose when Withrow came in, to his measured height, and sat again. Cael sat down at the house's table with *seventeen* under his left foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:161:*The seat opened at the second bell.* The clerk read the designation; Havel wrote the minute it ended. *The petitioner's procurator entered the petition.* Jent stood at his table with his leaf in one hand, read the petition's working paragraphs in an even voice that gave no one of them more weight than another, named his nine exhibits and sat; Havel wrote the minute he stood and the minute he sat. Nine minutes. *The respondent's counsel entered the answer.* She rose, small and grey with a steel rule in her hand, and summarized eleven pounds in a voice that did not hurry and did not linger; twenty-three minutes. Havel took the receipts for both lists of exhibits in the receipt book, nine items and a hundred and seventy-three, and wrote the numbers, and initialled them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:207:The house's counsel had her bag on her knees. "He tendered nine," she said. "I tendered a hundred and seventy-three. The junior receipted both lists, one after the other. Then the senior chair's minute went up under the hood and came back initialled, without either number changing the pace."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:153:He had known it would. It was the rule. At Gold weight the read took the air or the body, one or the other, never both; you laid the lane first and then you gave the read back, and it came back to you in a breath or two if you let it. He had no breath or two. He stepped into the lane's downhill with the read still out in the air somewhere and nothing to tell him where her points were but his own memory of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:165:Ten counts. It always came after a lane, since Norhold, and it came now: everything he owned answering a beat late. The right thing, slow. *Bill, not flag*, Lira had written in her palm on the honest floor. He tried to get his hands under him and they got there a beat after he asked. He tried to turn his head to find her and his neck turned a beat after that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:191:There was a cage round him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:199:He had been carrying it since the first exchange. He understood that now, kneeling. When his chest had found the point at his breastbone she had not only stopped him. She had laid a second point against him, against the cloth over Hesk's book, so lightly that he had taken it for the bruise of hitting the first one. It went where he went. It had gone with him on every push and every turn and down the camber. It was the first point of the cage, and every other point in the cage was laid from it, and to it, and through it, the way every stitch in a row of knitting is worked through the stitch before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:203:Three more, and it would close. He could feel exactly where. Three gaps, one at his left shoulder, one at his right hip, one over his head. When those three were laid, the cage would stop being a cage and start being a hold. A person inside a closed hold did not walk to a culvert. He was carried there, quite gently, by people who did not need to touch him at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:He knelt in the cage and held still, all of him, in front of four exchanges of her. That was all it had ever been. Not taking anything. He turned everything he had left toward what he had watched her do, and kept it there, open. Subtraction. The points laid where his half beat said he would go. The stillness of a woman threading a needle at the edge of her reach, half a second's price for range. The accumulation that never gave anything back. The stitch against his coat, and everything worked through it. He had paid for every one of those in his own body on this road, and he knew them now as well as he knew the stair to his own room with the lamps out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:225:Every course in the cut stood out to him as plainly as the chalk dust had stood out along his lane: taut grey threads in the air, from point to point to point, a great slack net laid down the whole length of the road, closed at both ends, open in one channel where Brom stood. He saw the nineteen round him, each with its line to the next. He saw the one against his coat, the first stitch, with every other line in the cage worked through it. And he saw how it was held. It was held from inside, from where it touched him. It was the same thing as the point at his breastbone and the one under the horse's leg and the ones that held the coachman's hands. It was a thing set against a surface and told to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:231:It went the way you let go of a breath you have been holding without knowing it. The point against his coat was simply not there any more. And because every other point in the cage had been laid through it, they went after it, one after another, in the order she had laid them, unthreading round him in a quick soft ripple like a row of stitches coming out when the first is pulled. The nineteen went. Their lines went out to the fourteen in the cut, and the fourteen went too. He felt them go down the road behind him like a rope paying out over a wheel: the three across the west mouth, the two in the south bank's lip, the three high in the north bank, the parapets, the milestone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:25:"There's a place tonight. The reading room, after supper. Karis has said yes and Brom's bringing the kettle and I've asked the kitchen for a honey cake, and they said yes because I didn't tell them who it was for." She picked up her spoon at last. "And I've asked Ephram."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:33:He had been seventeen for a year. It had been a long one. He had been seventeen at Norhold on the stone with a healer's fingers in his hand, and seventeen on the road home in a cart, and seventeen behind a bell-frame on a gatehouse roof, and seventeen five days ago on his knees in a chalk cut with a cage of points round him. And now he was not. Nothing in him had changed in the night. Nothing ever did, on a birthday. It was only a line in somebody's register moving down a row, but it was his line, and it had moved, and Lira had said it out loud across a refectory table before anybody else could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:65:Brom's spirit kettle stood on the end of the nearest shelf over its little blue flame, not boiling. Brom himself had the chair by the shelf, with his left arm in its sling across his chest and his long legs out in front of him. Lira had the window seat with her strapped foot up on a stool somebody had carried in from the annex across the landing. Karis stood by the table with her right hand unwrapped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:75:Ephram came at the half hour, out of breath, as if he had run up the library stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:85:Cael watched him see it. He watched the captain's eyes go along the long table, slowly, from end to end, over the turned stacks and the shut books and the squared backs of every sheet, the way they went along a floor before a bout, reading it. He saw Ephram understand that every piece of paper in the room had been turned face down, and that it had been turned face down because he was coming, and that nobody was going to say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:87:Ephram did not say so either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:93:"I'm comfortable," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:101:Cael watched him do it. He had watched Seln look at rooms for two years, at counters and sittings and in the long room at Ostrand from the one seat that saw every door, and he knew the order Seln looked at things in, because it was always the same. The door first: the brass lock, the frame, the card in its brass holder at eye height outside. Then the windows, both of them, the one over the window seat and the narrow one at the end, and what they looked out on, which was the dark and the top of the library's roof and, a long way below, the river. Then the landing behind him, and the annex door across it, and the stair going down. Then the table, with its field of turned backs. Then the people in it, one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:119:Nobody wanted to start. Lira said she could not sing and proved it in two notes. Karis said she knew no birthday songs, only the ones the founders' compilers had written down, and those were mostly about drowning. Ephram said, with his back to the table, that he had a captain's voice and not a singing one, which was the same thing said twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:123:He sang without getting up from the chair, with his sling across his chest and his eyes half shut, in a low voice that filled the small room easily, as if it were used to filling much bigger ones. It was not a birthday song. It was a harvest song from Velmere, he said afterward, that the estate's people sang at the last load every year, when the cart came into the yard with the last of the sheaves and the old women threw water on the horses for luck. It had a great many verses. Each one was about a field, and what had grown in it, and who had cut it. Brom sang every verse, slowly, all the way through. Nobody joined him, because nobody knew it, and nobody wanted him to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:125:At the last verse the tune changed. It went up, and came round again on itself, and there was a line at the end of it that everybody in Velmere evidently sang together, loudly, very nearly a shout. Brom sang it quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:127:Ephram, with his back to the table, sang it with him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:133:"The first-years," said Ephram. "Somebody on the ground floor has been singing those three lines under the residence windows since the night before the year opened. He's never once got the third line right. I thought it went like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:135:Cael sat at the table and looked at the back of Ephram's head and thought of the first night of the year, at his own window, with the house filling below him and somebody downstairs who could not learn a song.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:155:Ephram, with his back to the table, said, "Noted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:159:Then Ephram took the captain's file off his knees and opened it, without turning round, and took out three sheets of close-ruled paper, and held them up over his shoulder without looking, so that Cael could take them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:163:It was the chart. He had known it existed for a year and had never seen it close. Every figure he had put up on the house's floors since the qualifying year, in order, in Ephram's square careful hand, one to a line, with the date and the floor and the three cards and the figure, and a little dot beside each one on a running scale in the margin, so that the dots made a line down the three pages. The line breathed. It went up a little and down a little and never once settled, and never once went past the mark at the top that Ephram had ruled across all three pages in red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:167:"Ninety-one," said Ephram, to the window. "Ninety-one entries since the qualifying year, and one gap this week." He was quiet a moment. "I'm not asking about any of them. I've never asked and I'm not going to start tonight." He held out his hand over his shoulder again for the sheets. "I wanted you to know somebody's been paying attention. That's all. Many happy returns."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:169:Cael gave him the sheets back. Ephram put them in the file and shut it and folded his hands on top of it again, and looked at the window, and did not turn round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:193:"It's a very good bag," said Ephram, to the window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:195:The evening broke up a little before the tenth bell. Ephram went first, with the captain's file under his arm, backing out of the door so as not to see the table even at the last, and wished Cael many happy returns again from the landing, and went down the stair whistling the third line wrong. Brom took the kettle. Karis stayed to turn her papers face up again, one stack at a time, in the order she had turned them over, which she said was the only way she would ever find anything in the morning. Seln had gone before any of them, quietly, while the bag was being admired.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:221:The fountain went on in the court below. The glass of the window was cold, and the cold came through it onto the leather of the satchel where it stood against the pane, so that when he put his hand on the flap it was cold too, like a hand that has come in from outside. The ribs were a low steady ache. The arm under its binding itched, which the healer had said meant it was mending. The new thing in him was quiet. Once, when the latch of the window settled in its keeper as the wind moved the frame, it asked; and he told it *latch*, and it was quiet again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:57:He went down them in his head, as he went down them perhaps once a season, to be sure they were still where he had left them. A man who kept a mould room at a paper mill in the south. A woman who kept ledgers in a store-house in the west. A ferry-master on a coast he would never see again, who owed him a crossing and would have given him a boat. And an old officer of his own service, retired to a garden, who owed him the largest thing of the four and would have paid it gladly and been ruined by paying it. That one he would never spend. He had known that since the day it was owed. Some debts are only worth keeping so that the other man can sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:The seat wanted the maker of the copies to swear to them, and the advocate of record to sign beside his oath. Karis had said at supper that she would not put her name to a page she had not read against the thing it was copied from, and that this was not caution but manners. So the wing's original cards had come up the library stair in two shallow boxes under the porter's oilcloth, with Seln's leave and Bracken's receipt, and the second set of Seln's copies lay open beside them. He had made two of everything. One had gone down to the seat in the house's boards. The other had stayed in the annex, against exactly this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:119:"It's different," said the girl. She held the card closer to the lamp. "The copy's got twenty-two and two-thirds. The card's got…" She tilted it. "The card's got it twice. There's twenty-three and a third, and a line through it, and twenty-two and two-thirds written over the top in another ink."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:5:"Seln's put up next week's schedule," he said. "This morning, before the bell. A day early. He's never once put it up early." He set the tray on the end of the bed, carefully, and lowered himself into the chair, more carefully. His left arm was strapped across his chest in a sling the infirmary had made out of a triangle of linen and a great deal of disapproval. "There's one line in the First-day column. Ephram, the Crown yard, from the second bell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:9:"Gone over to the coming Fourth-day. Five floors, five names, a quarter of an hour apart, the same as before." Brom took a piece of bread off the tray and ate half of it. "And there's a new line against wing three. *Hesk-ward: excused (ribs).* In his fair hand. I watched three people read it and not one of them read anything into it but ribs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:11:"Ephram?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:13:"Ephram read it twice." Brom finished the bread. "Then he took his chart out of the captain's file and made a mark on it, standing at the board, and put it back. I don't know what mark. A gap, I'd guess. He's a man who writes down gaps."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:85:"*Bill, not flag*," she said, and stopped, and looked at what she had written, and rubbed it out with her thumb. "No. I don't know. It's not answering wrong. It's not answering late. It's not answering at all, it's just moving." She looked at him honestly. "I don't know which. I'll write that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:151:"Twelve seconds," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:173:Brom told her, and she listened. Then she came in, and sat on the floor with her back to the wall, and drew a line on the squared paper at the bell, and wrote beside it, in her wide crooked left-handed letters, *Doorway. Uncommanded. Twelve seconds. Brom counting.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:247:Somebody had shut the window in the night against the wind, and its latch had dropped into the keeper with a small iron click that he had heard in his sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:322:*Not on the leaf: the doorway. Twelve seconds, uncommanded, with Lira in it. Brom says that's the fire's and not the rule's. I'm writing it down so the next leaf has a line for doorways.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:He was a young man in a wet hat, and he apologised three times on the stair before he got to the door, and twice more inside it. The district seat's records room was a man short, he said. Two men, if you counted the one with the fever. A letter had come in from the registrar of the academy on the bluff, about an order and a seal, and it concerned the proceedings, and the senior records officer had said that the proceedings' own records support was in the town on its days off with nothing to do, and would the junior chair be so good as to check the registers and draft the usual.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:27:The first was the district's own impression book. It lived in the records room at the district seat, on a slanted desk of its own under the window, a book as tall as a child and as thick as a door-step. Every die issued for use in the district went into it on the day of issue, pressed into a red wafer and pasted on the page in the order it came, with the office's name and the date beside it in the keeper's hand. Havel turned its pages for two hours on the Third-day afternoon with the clerk in the wet hat hovering at his elbow, offering to help and being thanked. He found the seat's own seal on its page, forty years old, the river and the scale. He found the fee office's, and the bridge-ward's, and a ferry company's, and a hundred and eighty others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:171:It was a deep straight cutting through a spur of the hill that came down to the river. Somebody a long time ago had decided to go through the spur rather than round it, and had dug a road down into the chalk and carted the chalk away, and the walls were still there, white and steep and streaked with green where the water ran. On the north side, uphill, the bank stood twenty feet over the road. On the south side, toward the river, it was lower, ten or twelve feet. Toward the far end of the cut, a stone's throw short of where it opened out again, the south bank had been eaten away underneath by some flood long ago, before the road had a ditch, so that its lip hung out over a hollow of shadow, like a brow over an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:205:Together they made a frame. He saw its shape as he knelt there, as clearly as he had seen the chalk court on the oak. Both mouths of the cut were closed. The west mouth behind them had three points across it, and the east had the cart and the culvert and the milestone. The north bank was too high. The middle of the road was held. There was one way through the whole cut that the points did not close, and it was a channel along the foot of the south bank, under the overhanging lip, perhaps twelve feet wide, running east past the cart's tail to the culvert.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:233:He looked at the hobbles and the folded hood and the soft rope, and the staves that had been chosen because they bruised and did not cut, and the six faces that were not faces. He looked at the channel along the south bank, twelve feet wide, running to the culvert. He thought of the gully beyond it, which he could not see into, and what a man would keep there if he had come to take a person somewhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:"Registrar," he said. "If you would add that the evaluator of record was not told of any assessment proposed for the enrollee. Before it was proposed, or since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:57:"*Anything sealed, fetch the registrar,*" she read aloud. "You did, and you shut your window, and you stood where you could see the whole of it." She nodded to him, the nod she gave her senior staff. "Your board did very well this morning. Put a fresh coat on it in the spring, if you would. I should like it to last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:65:He had read her four times. The first two he already had: three paces in, onto the one level patch in the court. The third time he had asked the court what she could see from there, and the answer had been the arch. The gatehouse was twelve feet deep. From almost anywhere in the court you saw only a slice of the road through it, the left side from the covered walk, the right from the lecture range. There were a few feet of flag from which the whole opening lay open end to end, road and verges and the first bend below the gate. She had stood in them with her back three-quarters to the arch, and never once turned to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:101:Ephram put his tray down on Cael's right, halfway through. Then two of the cohort moved up from the far end. Then the rest of them shifted along, without anybody saying a word, until the table was the table again with no gap anywhere in it. "Grievances are Third-day evenings," Ephram said to nobody in particular, reaching for the bread. "I'm not taking any at lunch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:155:They had come out to the Crown yard with two lanterns and a lump of the porter's chalk. At the yard gate Cael had looked back and found that Seln had come too, unasked, a few steps behind them, with his coat buttoned to the chin and his hands in its pockets. Seln had not come onto the oak. He stood at its edge by the porter's boards, where the year's names stood chalked pale in the lamplight, Ephram's at the head of the Iron column, and watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:157:Cael had drawn the outer court at its own size, fifty feet by seventy, paced once by him and once by Brom until they agreed. The arch as two long lines, twelve feet apart. The lodge door in its cheek. The covered walk's corner. The mouth of the lane. The stable passage, with no door drawn across it. In the middle, a square for the capped well and a ring round it for the level flags; and Brom, before the chalk was out of Cael's hand, had walked into the ring three paces from the arch and turned his shoulders a hand's breadth toward the stable passage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:181:Brom considered the chalk arch, twelve feet deep, and the open stable passage. After a while he said, "I'd stand there too, if I was holding it. I'd never have found it in three steps."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:7:She had come up the annex stair at noon with the green volume open in both arms and a face like a girl who has broken something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:17:The first paragraph let the respondent's advocate of record put a question to the petitioner's procurator, through counsel, with the seat's permission, to be answered on the record and entered in the minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:21:Karis had spent four evenings in the digests after that, and come back with a date. The last recorded use of the first paragraph was a hundred and forty years old, in a river district, over a mill-race. She could find no recorded use of the second at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:27:The house's counsel came up the hill the following Second-day, and Karis put the green volume in front of her without a word, open at the page. The counsel laid her steel rule along the second paragraph first, before she had read the first at all, and kept it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:37:He had the ruled board under his arm and the door log open on the stand, as he had had it at every sitting since the first. He wrote Cael's line without needing to look: *Caelen Hesk-ward*, and in the standing column *the enrollee of record, unclassified*, nine seconds. He wrote the house's counsel by her name. Then he came to Karis, and stopped, with his pen over the standing column, and looked at a letter pinned to the top of his board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:43:*K. Dellenmoor. Advocate of record for the respondent, under the hearing provision.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:57:Jent, at the petitioner's table, had watched it go past.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:61:He meant it. That was the thing about Jent that Cael could never get used to. He meant every compliment he had ever paid in that room, and every one of them was true, and not one of them had ever cost him anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:65:When the production had been minuted, the house's counsel rose, and left her steel rule lying on the table behind her, pointing at the platform.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:69:"The respondent's advocate of record," said the counsel, "asks the seat's leave, under the hearing provision, to put one question through counsel to the petitioner's procurator, to be answered on the record."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:73:Then the clerk bent to the green volume at his elbow, which was the same edition as the annex's, cracked in the same place, and found the page, and set his finger on it, and passed it up under the hood. The hands under the grey read it without turning a leaf. They passed it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:She had not lifted her head since the seat opened. Through the production, through the receipt, through Jent's compliment, her pencil had gone steadily along her file and her eyes had stayed on the page. Now the pencil stopped. Her head came up. She looked across the floor at the petitioner's table, and kept looking at it, and did not look down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:91:Jent was on his feet before the breath was finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:131:Jent came down the steps behind them at his ordinary pace, with his clerk and his tabbed folder. At the bottom he stopped, and put his hat on, and touched its brim to Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:141:She said it quietly, almost to herself, and Cael heard what was under it, because he had felt the same thing at the respondent's table with the schedule on his knee. Every other person in that room had been careful all year. The house's counsel weighed every sentence on her steel rule. Bracken read every seal twice. Withrow had ordered the production answered in the house's own boards. Karis herself had changed one word on one card four times. And the man on the other side of the floor had been asked the one question in the whole proceeding that ought to have made him careful, and he had answered it the way you answer a child who asks you which way is north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:145:In the coach going home, Karis took the plain card out of her wallet, the one with her question on it, the word changed four times. Under the question, in her finding hand, she wrote Jent's answer, word for word. She dated it. Then she opened her notebook and slid the card inside its front cover, in among the things she kept there: the fielding clause in her finding hand, the squad rule, the charter's slip, and the stratum note with its question mark still waiting. She shut the book on all of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:147:The house's counsel came up the hill with them that afternoon instead of taking her chaise home. She wanted to go through the morning's minute with Karis in the annex, line by line, while it was fresh, and to check the production against the receipt the junior chair had written, and she said she trusted her memory of neither past a night's sleep. Bracken had the guest room over the records hall made up for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:181:Withrow came round the corner of the lecture range at the pace she used for every corridor in her house, with Bracken a half-step behind her and the house's counsel a step behind him, buttoning her coat, with her hair not quite pinned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:185:The lodge-man had painted it himself in white on black, years before Bracken came to the hill, and he went over the letters every spring with a fine brush. *CARTS — ROUND BY THE LANE. CALLERS — SIGN AND WAIT. POST — THE BAG ON THE HOOK. ANYTHING SEALED — FETCH THE REGISTRAR.* Cael watched Withrow find the last line with her eyes and stop on it. The lodge-man had done exactly what his board said. Anything sealed had come up the road, and he had sent his boy for the registrar, and he had shut his window so that he could stand in his own door and see the whole of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:He sat on his heels behind the bell-frame and heard the name at the top of it go round the court. *Caelen Hesk-ward.* Nobody who knew him had ever said it to his face. It was the name the registry kept for him, the way a shop keeps a number on a parcel, and here it was coming up off the flags of his own gate in a stranger's voice, with an order underneath it. *Attendance. Transport and escort provided. Produce the enrollee.* And at the bottom, the five words he had first read on the morning of the eighth day at the foot of a challenge's first page, and had read at the foot of every registry instrument since, and had never heard spoken by anybody at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:217:Nobody spoke. Four riders, a chancellor, the house's counsel with her hair coming down, the lodge-man in his doorway, and up on the gatehouse a boy behind a bell-frame, all of them stood and waited while the registrar read a seal with his eyes shut. It took him a minute and forty seconds. Cael counted it. It was a long time to stand still in a court; Bracken had earned every second of it at his counter, and the riders seemed to know that, because none of them shifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:43:"*Memorandum to keepers of stations and to clerks of entry. Corrective classification at Kindling evaluation.*" She put her finger under the line on the newest leaf. "And the body. It's three sentences, and it's been three sentences since this one." She touched the yellowest. "A word changes here and there, and *shall* became *is to*. A clause about the post came in when the post did. Otherwise you could lay the oldest on top of the newest and read straight through them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:He could not help it. Under every seal there was a line of them, three or four to an issue, and he went down them the way he went down the names on a bout card, by habit, and without meaning to he had counted. Nineteen issues and seventy-one names, give or take two he could not read in the yellow ones where the ink had gone brown and spread. Officers of the registry, by the titles after them: a chief clerk of entry, a keeper of the rolls, a second secretary, men and women whose offices Bracken would have known and Cael had never heard of. Not one of them appeared on two issues: ten years at a desk, and then a new name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:95:"*Of the advisory corrections function. The function is appended to the reading and is not the reading. It does not alter what the reading shows. It advises what is to be entered, and the entry is made as advised. It is to be maintained, and its maintenance is the office's charge.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:117:He thought of Denvash, which he did not often let himself do. A district office on a cold morning, a chalk circle on boards worn into two shallow places where a generation of feet had stood, and a warden-adjunct with tired grey eyes three feet away at a desk with a brass plate let into it. Something had woken behind his breastbone, small, a point of light at the edge of his sight that he could feel and never point to. He had counted eleven seconds while the plate did not light, and then the word had come, very far away and very close, and the plate lighting late with the same word in it, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:139:Lira's hands had come together in her lap, one inside the other, and stayed there. Karis had not taken her gloves off. She sat with them resting on the cloth either side of the leaf, as if she had forgotten they were on. Brom's good hand had gone, very slowly, to the left side of his coat, and lay flat over the inside pocket where the summons was, and the docket, and the letter from Velmere that he had carried sealed since the forty-fifth day. He did not take anything out. He only kept his hand there, the way a man puts his hand on a wall in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:167:"A corridor," he said, "in a district station, a year on, with green distemper on the walls to the height of a man's shoulder, and benches. I was waiting for a file. A keeper came out of the frame room with a mother and a girl, and he was pleased, the way they are when a reading is a good one, and I heard him tell the mother that her girl read strong, and read into a second family as well, and he had not seen it in years." He looked at the father's letter, folded under its clip in the third row. "A week later I was back for another file, and the station's out-tray was on the counter, and her certificate was in it, waiting to go. One family, first rank." He took his hand off the table. "That was when the paper turned into children. It had been only paper for a year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:77:*The keeper is invisible. The witness is a location.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:87:The keeper is invisible. He had been invisible for fifteen years. The boy's people had never been invisible for a day. All year they had walked about the hill in the open and let themselves be counted, the girl on the walls with her chalk and the big one at the frames and the researcher with her lamp lit in the top window every night where the whole valley could see it. Five days ago, at the gate, the house itself had stood in the middle of its own court and read the paper out loud. Nobody on that side had hidden anything. They had simply been there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:95:He had counted the boy for a year. Two slips on a common-room fire. A season of figures that never once settled into a line. An afternoon at Norhold where everything held back went out in one breath, on purpose, in front of eight thousand people. He had counted all of it, and filed none of it, and somewhere in the counting, without asking his leave, it had turned into the other thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:115:At the residence door a knot of third-years was standing out of the wet arguing about a bout, and he went round the edge of them: seven to twelve. On the stair, coming down, the Current girl from Rooke's program with her wet hair in a towel, who flattened herself to the wall to let him by and said, "Sorry, sorry," as if the stair were hers to be sorry about: thirteen. Two more on the first landing. Three in the corridor, one of whom was the captain, Ephram, with his green-taped file under his arm, who nodded to him and went on: eighteen. Five more behind half-open doors as he passed, reading or mending or asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:147:"It has Bracken's card on the door. *Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.* It has the brass lock and one key, and she wears the key. The old lumber room across the landing has the code on a shelf and the assistant's table, and the house has called the two rooms together the annex since Bracken had it cleared, and people go up and down that stair all day with books." He stopped. "And it's hers. Nobody on the hill would think twice about four people going up to her room. They've been doing it all year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:167:"There'll be six names in the First-day column," he said. "There always are. Ephram has the Crown yard from the second bell every First-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:209:"Since the gate, no enrollee of this house leaves the hill alone," she said. "You know that. Tomorrow you are five, and the house's counsel went down to her rooms by the wharf this afternoon while the bridge was still open, and will meet you at the side door." She looked along the table at each of them, the way she looked at a page before she signed it. "I would go with you, if the seat had asked for me. It has not. It has asked for him, and the maker of the copies, and the advocate of record. Go the long way and come back the short one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:233:He told them in the order it had happened, because that was the only fair way: the knock at dusk, the case set down beside the book, *I'm going to open this. Not tonight.* What he had asked and what Seln had answered. The reading room. The floor schedule and the First-day after the sitting, five names, five quarters of an hour, and a sixth line that was only Ephram wanting the Crown yard. He did not say what was in the case, because he did not know. Nobody asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:122:*The bills: Lira, the right ankle (the ditch, not the bursts; she wants it written that way). Brom, the left shoulder, out and in. Karis, the right hand, the fourth. Me, the left forearm, the right ribs. The coachman's near horse. Seln, nothing anyone saw. The leaf said Brom for the minute. Brom was there for the minute.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:138:The bar was a long white pole on a pivot across the road, where the river road came in off the flats to the west gate. The coachman stopped for the keeper to raise it. The keeper came out of his box with his hand out for the toll, and the bar went up, and swung, and came to rest against its post. And as it touched the post, something in Cael said, very quietly and with great courtesy, *There?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:146:The offer went away at once, without argument, the way a clerk goes away when you shake your head. The keeper took his toll and the coach went under the bar and on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:172:"Think how it reads," she said. "Not how it happened. How it reads, on a minute, at a seat, in that room. The respondent arrives at a documentary sitting the seat asked for him in person, with chalk to his knees and his advocate's hand in a wet cloth. He says that on the way here, this morning, on the only road open, six people he cannot name, whose faces he did not see, with no paper and no seal and no office, tried to take him into custody, and failed, and left. His witnesses are the four people who came with him and a coachman in the house's pay." She stopped. "No faces. No names. No paper. No wagon, by tonight, I'd wager, if anybody went to look. On the morning of a sitting, six days after the house turned away an order at its gate and made the order look sinister." She did turn then, and looked at all of them. "It reads as manufactured theater. Jent wouldn't even have to say so. He'd be kind about it. Everybody in the room would be kind about it, and every weekly in four towns would print it by the Fourth-day under a woodcut of the gate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:198:The house's counsel was waiting just inside, in her grey coat, with her bag and her steel rule. She had come up from her rooms by the wharf in the morning before the water reached the market steps. She looked at them as they came in out of the light, one at a time, the way she read a paper, from the end up: at Lira on Brom's arm with one foot strapped; at Brom's left arm hanging; at Karis's hand; at Cael's sleeve; at the chalk on every one of them to the knee, white as millers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:234:"The advocate of record."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:240:Jent had sat at his table through all of it with his spectacles on their black cord and his travelling inkwell shut, and said nothing, because a documentary sitting gave the petitioner nothing to say. As the long room emptied he gathered his single folder and came along behind their table toward the door, and stopped by Cael's chair for a moment, and looked at the chalk on Cael's sleeve and on Lira's strapped boot and on all their knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:248:He did it out of habit, the way he had read Jent at the conference and at every sitting since, four times, and found nothing four times. He found nothing now. The man stood there in his brown coat with the mended cuffs, middle height and middle years, with a pleasant remark about the weather on his face, and there was nothing on him at all. There was no weight laid down anywhere, and no grain of hiding. It was not that Jent was hiding what he knew. It was that there was nobody in the coat for the read to find, only a procurator of record, doing the job he had been sent to do, courteously.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:254:Jent nodded, as if that were the right answer to something, and went out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:260:He had believed in it. He saw that now, sitting in it, the way you see the shape of a thing only when you are about to put it down. All year he had believed in this room. Not the hood, and not Jent, but the room: the text on the clerk's table, and the code in its three volumes, and the provisions with their dates in them, and the minute that went up under the hood and came back initialled. He had written it into Hesk's book on the first night of the year in his best hand. *The system has text, and text has held me every time the people failed.* He had stood in a dairy with the slate wall behind him and defended it at full strength. He had brought receipts to this room all year, and when the room would not take them he had gone on bringing them, because the room was the system and the system had text.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:268:He did not feel anything break. He had expected, if it ever came, that it would break like a bone, with a noise. It went like the cage had gone on the road: one stitch, and then all the rest after it, quietly, in the order they had been laid. Every receipt he had ever counted was still true. Every one of them was still in the book. They simply did not hold anything up any more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:5:He lay on his back with the grey of the window on the ceiling and waited for it, the way a man waits for a dog in the next room to bark once it has heard him stir. The ribs told him where they were. The forearm under its dressing itched in a long line from the wrist to the elbow, which the healer said was the skin remembering how to be skin. The latch sat in its keeper and the door stood in its frame, and nothing in him leaned toward either of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:59:It ran down the whole of the door: four floors and their hours for the week, the Crown yard, the second floor, the old frames and the east hall, and against each hour a name or a house or a program, a hundred lines of it in a hand so even that nobody had ever once looked at it twice. On the Fourth-day, after supper, five lines sat a quarter of an hour apart against five different floors. One of them was his. *Hesk-ward. Old frames.* Under the Fifth-day it still said, in the same hand, *Hesk-ward: excused (ribs).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:61:Ephram was reading the board when he came in. Ephram read the board every morning, all of it, from the top, the way other people read a letter from home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:121:The top landing had one lamp on a bracket, and the annex door stood shut across it. The reading room's door had Bracken's card in its brass frame at eye height. *Reading room. Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:131:"The annex floor," said Karis, before he could ask. "All of it, in order, and I'll be a week putting it back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:157:Seln stood on the landing with his coat buttoned to the throat and something square under it, and he did not come in. He stood in the doorway and looked at the room in the order Cael knew. The door, the lock, the card; the two windows, the lit one with its lamp, the narrow one at the end, dark, with the library's roof below it; the landing behind him, the annex door, the stair; the bare table and the shaded lamp; then the four of them, one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:197:"The first sheet is a copy of a station's Kindling record," said Karis. She read it in her plain voice, the one she used for a citation, giving each word its weight and no more. "*River station, the fourth district. Entry of the twelfth. A girl of the town. Reading at the frame: Current, second rank. Keeper's note at the margin: reads into Mire. Strong.*" She laid it face up in front of her. "That's the keeper's hand copied. You can see where he pressed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:Karis took the second pair, and read the record, and Brom read the order. A station in the hill country, a boy. *Ash, first rank. Keeper's note: something of Stone in the frame; second family?* And the order: *Enter: Ash, Rank One. Margin reading not to be entered. Observe nine weeks.* And on the back, *Observed nine weeks. Nothing to report.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:213:The third was a coastal station, a girl again. *Current, second rank at the frame. Keeper's note at the margin: reads into Wind, faint.* Enter: Current, Rank One.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:217:Every keeper had written something at the margin: a second family, or a note that the frame had reached past the column it was meant to stay in. *Reads into. Something of. Faint.* And every order took the margin away and wrote down only the column. Twice now a rank had gone too, but the rank was not the thing; the margin was the thing. He could see it as plainly as he could see the clip, and he did not say it. It was Karis's to say, when she had it in order, and she would not thank anybody for saying it first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:253:"*To the clerk of the district office. Sir. I am the father of the girl entered at the river station on the ninth. I do not write to complain and I hope you will not take it so. At the frame the keeper told us she read strong and read into a second family besides, and he said he had not seen it often, and shook my hand. Now the paper has come and it says only the one, and the first rank. I expect there has been an error in the copying, which I know is easy done. I do not mind which it is. She is a good girl either way. Only she asks me, and I do not know which to tell her. If you would write and say which, I would be grateful. Your servant.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:265:"*I have looked at the station's sheet. The keeper's reading is on it, and it is not what the registry entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:129:Lira had forgotten that until she let herself into the east hall after the tenth bell on Rooke's key, alone, with one lantern, and stood inside the great door with her hand on its edge until her eyes had learned the dark. The shutters were down, and the painted crosses went away from her across the boards, white, then grey, then nothing. Somewhere at the far end the heavy frame ticked on its springs as the cold got into them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:197:She had wondered about that, once. Only once, on the second night, on the stair. What had the keeper read on her, before anybody wrote it down? She remembered a brass plate, and cold fingers turning her by the shoulder, and being told to stand still. She did not remember anybody saying a word she could use.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:Cael heard it, because he was watching her. She had come off the end of a long flat curve and put her palm on the man exactly where she had meant to, the best touch she had made all season, and the steward said "Touch. Halcenvane" in his ordinary voice, and the north benches, which ten minutes ago had been on their feet for the house's Wind, did not make a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:21:*The respondent institution is directed to produce at the fourth sitting of the seat the complete records of evaluation-linked floor work of the enrollee of record, Caelen Hesk-ward, for the academic year to date, in the form in which they are kept.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:137:Zerin's card at the end of last year. Four words and two stops. *Silver bracket. Next cycle.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:7:"I have been an evaluator of record eleven times," he said, to the coach in general, as they crossed the bridge. "I have never before been asked for the notes. The figures, yes. The minute, always. Not the notes." He considered it. "I find I do not mind. I should mind very much if I had written them differently for being read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:13:The young officer at the side door wrote him in the log under *standing* as *evaluator of record to the respondent's enrollment*, and Gault leaned over the board and read it upside down, and nodded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward," said the seat's clerk. "Upon the merits. The eighth sitting. Production, under the seat's order of the sixth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:19:The house's counsel rose, and said that the house produced, and sat, which was all she was needed for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:21:"The evaluator of record," said the clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:45:"*Seventy-sixth day. An order was presented at the house's gate, by four riders, in the name of an office styled facilities, for the enrollee's attendance upon an assessment. No assessment of this enrollee has been notified to the evaluator of record, by any office, at any time. I have written that down.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:49:Jent had risen, and stood with his hands at his sides and his spectacles hanging, and for the first time in eight sittings Cael thought he saw the man choose his words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:51:"The petitioner has no knowledge of the matter in that note," said Jent. "I say so for the minute, and I say it carefully. It is no part of the petition. No instrument of the kind described was sought by the petitioner or issued at his instance." He paused. "As to the rest of the volume: the petitioner accepts it, as he said he would. He relies on none of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:103:"To find a leaf? In the annex, off the shelf, eleven seconds. I had the assistant time me." She pulled a face. "Out of oilcloth, on my knees, the first day: four minutes and some. And I tore a corner. Yesterday, fifty seconds. I want it under thirty, and I want it under thirty with cold hands, so I've had the window open since noon."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:175:He had known it was the last since the First-day, when the floor sheet came across the counter with a line on it that he had not seen before in two years of floor sheets. *Supervised hours under the provision, the academic year: complete upon this session.* It was in the desk clerk's hand, with the wing's stamp. The provision asked for a number of hours signed by the evaluator of record, and he had been paying them down every week since the year opened, a bout at a time. This one closed the account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:221:Ephram was at the rail under the windows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:231:It was short. Cael could see that much from forty feet, from the way the pencil moved: four or five words, and then, after a pause in which Ephram looked out of the high window at nothing, two or three more underneath. He did not hide it; he simply wrote it facing the wall. When he had done he read it over once, and his face, which was a good plain open face that had never been much use for keeping things in, did something Cael had not seen it do before. It went still. It was the face of a man who has been adding a long column for two years and has reached the foot of it and written the sum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:237:"That's the year," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:241:"Ninety-eight, and two holes." Ephram tucked the file under his arm. "And not one of them over the line. I ruled that line in the first month I knew you. I thought I'd have to move it." He looked at Cael then, level, the captain's look, the one he gave a first-year who had told him half of something. It went on a moment longer than was comfortable. "I'm not asking," he said. "I told you on your birthday I wasn't, and I'm not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:249:Cael stood at the rail where the sheets had lain, and the wood was still a little warm from Ephram's forearm. Whatever had been written on the back of the third page was in a green-taped file going down the wing's corridor, and he did not know what it was. He knew only that Ephram had decided something, and that it had taken him ninety-eight entries to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:257:A single leaf of registry stock, under the seat's seal at Ostrand, the river and the scale. He knew the form. He had answered it, in one shape or another, perhaps two hundred times in forty years, and it had never once before taken him more than the time it takes to dip a pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:259:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. The evaluation seat constituted for these proceedings, having first given notice in the year's eighth week that certification would be required, now renews its request to the Archmarshal's office for certification of the standard protocol, as applied to a re-evaluation of the enrollee of record at Halcenvane, should such be ordered. The seat's closing sittings are set down for the year's last month. The office's certificate is requested before the first of them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:27:"He verifies it," she said. "He's an evaluator; it's the whole of what he is. He doesn't believe a thing because it's on a table; he measures it. So he goes home and pulls a frame reading, or a station sheet, or a fresh reading on one of the forty, to see whether the case is true." She turned her pale fingers over on the cloth. "And the frame's theirs, and the station's theirs, and the reading runs through the function. Every instrument he could check it with comes back and says the case is wrong. And he believes his instruments, because he's believed them for forty years. And somewhere it gets minuted that a forgery was offered to the Archmarshal by persons connected with the respondent at Halcenvane."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:33:"He believes it," she said, "all of it, at once, the way you'd want him to, and it breaks him." She was looking at the case. "He's been measuring true for forty years because he thought true was the point. We show him the point was never true. And he goes home, and sits at his desk, and does nothing, because there's nothing he can do. He signs what he's given, or he doesn't, and either way he's a broken man signing things." She lifted her chin. "And we'd have done that to him. We'd have spent the one good man in the whole building for a bad night's sleep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:91:*Four streets back from the seat, on the wharf side. The tall house with the brass plate. The third floor. The second bell after noon. Neither office's. Nothing written.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:97:"Is borrowed." Seln very nearly smiled. "A records administrator's, on the third floor of a leasehold building four streets back from the registry seat. He is on circuit in the hill stations for six weeks. His landlord's keeper lends the room by the morning or the afternoon to anybody with a reason and a deposit, and writes the reason in a book, and does not read it twice. My reason is a reading." He looked at the note. "I have used that kind of room before, but never that one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:111:The lane was narrow and steep, with a gutter down the middle and the backs of the warehouses on one side and a row of tall old houses on the other, let out floor by floor to clerks and factors and men who did a trade in paper. The building was the fourth along, with a door with a brass plate beside it listing its tenants, and a keeper's window, and a stair. Seln was at the keeper's window when Cael came in, writing his reason in the book, and did not look round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:123:Brom had the chair with its back to the wall. Karis had the chair at the table's end, with the case on the floor at her feet, its lock still shut; Seln had carried it down inside the post satchel, between the wing's letters, and given it to her in the keeper's hall without a word. Seln came up last and locked the door behind him and gave the key to nobody. He did not sit. He went to the window and stood a little to one side of it, where the frame hid him from the lane, and looked down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:133:"He came in by the front, not the yard. He walked up the lane from the wharf end, on the gutter side, in grey, with nothing in his hands, and he stopped and read the brass plate by the door, and then he went in past the keeper's window, where the keeper could see him." Seln looked at the door. "By the clock on the landing, eleven minutes early."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:215:"The third is certification." For the first time he looked at Karis, only at Karis, and Cael understood that the not looking at himself was being chosen, and chosen again, every moment. "Before this year is out the seat will send to my desk for my certificate on the protocol for a re-evaluation at Halcenvane. I have known it was coming since the convening. I meant to sign it, because the protocol is sound, and in forty years I have never refused my name to a sound protocol." He lifted his hand off the table. "When it comes, I shall decline. It is the only sentence I have left that they still need."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:237:Seln turned the key for him. He went out onto the landing, and they heard him take the stair the way he had come up it, one even step after another, down past the old clock in its box and the keeper's window and out by the front door into the lane, where anybody at all could see him go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:255:At the fourth station, a day upriver from Ostrand, the keeper had walked him through the frame room as keepers always did, proud of his brass, and at the end of it had opened a long drawer under the counter to show him the station's own sheets for the year. A keeper kept his sheets as a miller kept his tallies. They were the station's private record of what its frame had read on each child, before any clerk of entry had touched it, and this keeper wrote a good hand and wanted it seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:257:"Every one since I came, sir," the keeper had said. "Margins and all. My predecessor never wrote the margins. I always have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:259:The Archmarshal had stood at the open drawer for what must have seemed to the keeper a very ordinary moment. The sheets lay in their order, a year of them, the top one in the keeper's careful hand. He could see from where he stood that there was something written at its margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:261:He had not put his hand in the drawer. He had said that it was a very well-kept drawer, which it was, and that the keeper did his station credit, which he did, and he had watched the man close it with his own hands and turn the little key in it. Then he had signed the inspection book and gone out into the rain to the next station, and he had not been able to say afterward what the next station's frame looked like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:281:He would not write the man down. He would not write any of them down: not the girl with the turned ankle who had asked him a straight question, not the big one with the bad shoulder, not the researcher who had held out her gloves, not the boy he had not let himself look at. A description is a file with the name left off, and he had spent forty years learning how easily the name comes back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:289:He could send to the station for its own sheet. A keeper writes at the margin of his own sheet, whatever the clerk of entry is told to enter, and the district clerk in the river town had said so in four lines. The sheet would be there, in a drawer in the north, with the keeper's hand on it. But a request from the Archmarshal's office for a station sheet goes in the dispatch ledger downstairs in his clerk's tidy upright hand, with a number and a date and a name, which would be her name. Then she would be a file, and somebody somewhere would read the ledger. There would be an order, clipped to a copy, and nine weeks of somebody watching her, and a slip on the back of it with nothing on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:He came in by the yard door with a single sheet in his hand and his spectacles pushed up on his forehead, and stood a moment inside it while his eyes found the three of them: Ephram on the long frame, in the middle of a set; Brom beside the short one with his arms folded; Cael against the wall. The old frames stood in a low stone room behind the stables that had been a harness store once and still smelled of it, with two lamps, a sanded floor, and the two great timber frames with their iron bars and springs, older than anybody on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:23:Then Ephram, who had not stopped his set, finished it, and let the long frame's bar come back to its rest with both hands, and went to the rack by the wall where the short frame's iron plates hung on their pegs. He took down two, and looked at them, and hung one back, and took down a heavier one in its place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:35:The short frame was a simple machine: two timber uprights, a crossbar of iron that ran in greased slots between them, and under each end of the bar a spring as thick as a man's wrist. You stood beneath the bar and took it on your shoulders, or on your forearms crossed above your head, and somebody let the catches go, and the springs drove it down. A sigil frame at a district seat was a finer thing, with a plate that read what the practitioner's Path did under the weight. This one read nothing. It only pressed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:41:Ephram let the catches go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:43:The bar came down on Brom's crossed forearms with a sound like an anvil taking a hammer. The springs sang, and Brom's knees gave an inch and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:51:Ephram counted under his breath, and at thirty he threw the catches, and the bar went up, and Brom stepped out from under it and let his arms down, and the skin went back to being skin, wrists last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:55:Ephram took a third plate off the rack, a small one, and held it up, and raised his eyebrows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:59:Ephram hung it on the left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:61:They did it eleven times, by Cael's count, in the better part of an hour, and in all that time neither of them said another word that was not a number or a side. Ephram put the plates on and took them off, and watched Brom's left elbow the whole while with the level, patient attention he gave everything he meant to be right about. Twice he shook his head at nothing Cael could see, and took a plate off, and Brom did not argue. Once he added one that Brom had not asked for, and Brom looked at it and walked in under the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:67:"The left's back," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:79:He did not say what he would do with the finding afterward, but his hand went to the pocket again and rested there. Ephram, at the rack, with his back to them, hung up the last plate and squared it on its peg, and if he saw the hand he gave no sign of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:151:The lath was there. It came across level, a hand above the boards, at the place where her right ankle would be when the weight went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:179:*Advancement evaluation, Silver bracket. The district seat, Ostrand. The first sitting after the year's close. Program's entrant: the Wind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:185:"That's after the ruling," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:251:That was all. He did not seem to think it needed more, and after a moment Cael understood that it did not. Brom had held twelve feet of road with one arm, and had sat a rota outside a door for two nights without being asked. Of course it was Brom who counted, and counted again when the first count was right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:75:Karis came in late with her coat buttoned wrong and a volume of the code under her arm, and sat, and opened the volume beside her bowl, and read it while she ate without once looking at what she was eating. Brom came in after her with Ephram, and the two of them sat at the end of the table with their elbows touching and ate enough for six and said almost nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:77:Cael had not known Ephram was staying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:79:"Somebody has to keep the frames honest," said Ephram, when he asked. "The springs go if nobody works them for a fortnight. I've seen it." He wiped his bowl with his bread. "Also I like the hill better with nobody on it. Don't tell the cohort."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:95:He went to the frames in the end, because Brom and Ephram were there, and stood at the wall and watched them work. Ephram ran his sets on the long frame, one after another, in the measured way he did everything, while Brom stood beside the short frame with his arms folded and watched the springs. Once, Brom put his good hand on the short frame's bar and pressed, and felt it give, and nodded, and took the hand away. Neither of them spoke for the better part of an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:121:He got out paper and wrote back the same afternoon, before he could think better of it. He told Hesk that the hill was empty, and described the refectory's one table and the fountain you could hear, and Brom and the captain at the frames, keeping the springs honest. He wrote that Lira's ankle was mending, and that it rained most days, and that Karis had read the whole of a code volume over her breakfast without spilling a drop on it, which Hesk would think was the most remarkable thing in the letter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:135:"The second paragraph," she said, before anybody asked. "*Of the seat's hearing, and who may be heard.* The advocate of record heard at the close, by the seat's leave. The house's counsel thinks the seat will give it, if it's asked properly and early." She squared the first row with the side of her hand. "So I'm writing a closing. In bones, for now. The flesh after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:15:The bridge was open, the river running brown and quick a long way under its second ring, and they went up the market street at a walk. There were perhaps a dozen people on the four steps of the wool exchange this morning, where once there had been thirty, and only two of them had folding boards. A merits sitting, the house's counsel had said, was a thing correspondents came to on the first day and the last. In between, it was paper, and paper did not sell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:19:The young officer at the side door had got over his cold at last, and he had the log open on its board, and the first name already written, in the hand of a man who had written it so often that he no longer saw it. Cael read it upside down as he went past, as he had read it at every sitting since the fortieth day. *Caelen Hesk-ward.* And in the second column, under *standing*: *the enrollee of record, unclassified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:21:The officer wrote Karis's line under it without being told how. *Advocate of record for the respondent, under the hearing provision.* She had been in his book under that title since the fourth sitting, and he had stopped looking up to check the spelling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:23:The house's counsel was waiting inside, small and grey and straight-shouldered, with her bag in one hand and her steel rule in the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:43:He knew what was in the case on Karis's shelf, and that this room ran on it. The frame they meant to stand him in was the thing on the leaf, appended to the reading and not the reading, and every person on that platform would go home tonight not knowing it, and so would Jent, perhaps, and so would the clerk. He was the only one in the long room, besides Karis at his elbow, who knew what the argument was about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:47:"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward," said the seat's clerk. "Upon the merits. The sixth sitting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:49:Jent stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"It follows," said Jent, "that the second schedule to the petition is wrong in one particular. It suggests, at its ninth line, that the first renewal was sealed out of its order. It was not. It was sealed nine days after the panel sat, because an officer of an inspecting delegation held the registry's copy for his countersignature, as he was entitled to do, and the respondent's advocate has shown the seat the very leaf." He took one sheet from the folder after all, and held it out to the clerk. "The petitioner withdraws that line, and tenders the schedule again without it. I should not like the seat to rule upon a schedule with a mistake in it. Least of all one of mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:67:"And lastly," said Jent. "A great deal was said at the preliminary about the evaluator of record at Halcenvane. I said some of it. I should like it in this minute, in plain words, that the petitioner accepts that officer as competent in every particular, accepts his panels as properly held, and accepts every figure he has signed as honestly come by." He let his hands fall. "The petition relies upon none of these matters. It never has. I mention them only so that nobody is put to the trouble of proving what I do not dispute."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:75:He looked for it, quite honestly. They had spent four days on that table of renewals, in the annex, at the start of the year. He had found the nine days himself, in his own old Log, and Karis had let him find them. He had been proud of it for a week. And a man in a mended coat had just stood up before a hood and said that every line of it was right, and adopted it, and thrown away a piece of his own petition rather than leave an error standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:77:It was a clean win. It was as clean as anything in the whole proceeding. And it weighed nothing, because Jent had not needed the ground he was giving. He was a man clearing a table before a meal. Every concession made the petition smaller and harder, until there would be nothing left of it but the single thing it had always been: a question for the seat's discretion, about a frame.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:89:"The seat notes the petitioner's acceptance of the evaluator of record. It will nonetheless have his notes before it. The respondent's house is ordered to produce that officer's notes upon the enrollee for the current academic year, entire, at the eighth sitting. So ordered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:101:Jent overtook them on the steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:103:The rain had thinned to a mist, and the market street ran shining down toward the river with the carts standing in it. Withrow had gone ahead to her carriage and bowed to nobody, and Bracken was at the coach door with the seal. Jent came down the four steps behind the house's party with his one folder under his arm, and at the bottom he stopped beside Karis and waited until she had to look at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:127:"We won three," he said, and made himself hear it as he said it. The nine days. Four days in the annex, and his own old Log, and her pencil stopping when he found it. "The table's in the minute now as the petitioner's own. Nobody can ever go behind it. Whatever they rule, they rule knowing every renewal was good."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:189:He took out the six sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:203:Everything else about the line, the registers answered perfectly. Second schedule, fourteenth line, received on the fortieth day, re-tendered on the hundred and seventeenth, both leaves alike. There was nothing there to write. The six sheets held what the registers could not answer. This, they could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:5:Nobody saw her work; that was the arrangement. The assistant went home at the sixth bell every evening with a story about glue, and believed it, and told Ephram, who told the refectory, so that by the Fourth-day half the hill knew that the researcher at the top of the library was mending a founding volume with something that smelled, and kept away. The lamp burned in the window at its usual height every night and went out at its usual hour. Behind it, at the far end of the long table under the brown-paper shade, Karis sat with the case open and the two keys in the room, Seln's for an hour at a time while he sat silent at the middle of the bench, and laid the whole of it out again, and again, and again, until it would lie in the order she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:33:"I sorted them by the margin," she said, "not by the station or the date, but by what the keeper wrote at the edge of the reading, that the order told the clerk not to enter. And every margin is the same kind of thing. *Reads into. Something of. A second family, faint.* Not one keeper in forty wrote at the margin that a child was too strong. Several of the forty were weak; a first rank, and a low one, with a second family showing faintly behind it like a face behind a window. They were corrected all the same. And I went through every pair looking for one where the order took strength away and left the rest, and there isn't one. When a rank goes, it goes because the second family went, and the second family was what was holding the rank up." She moved one finger on the cloth. "They don't correct how much. They correct how far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:129:"That's the word your Arbiter said at Denvash," said Karis, quite gently. "It isn't a classification, Cael, and it never was. It's what they wrote where the old name used to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:177:For the fourth she did not unfold the slip at all. She took the grey notebook out of her coat instead and opened it at the inside of its front cover, where a few lines had gathered over two years that belonged to no case: the fielding clause, the squad rule, the charter's slip, Jent's answer on its plain card, and above them all, in her small hand, the line from the first night of the year. Cael knew it before she put her finger under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:203:"There were people," said Seln. "Two hundred years ago, one, at a desk. Since then there have been seventy-one people signing for coal, and keepers writing down margins, and clerks entering what they're told, and evaluators stamped *enter at file*, and a district clerk who told a father the truth and sent it up. None of them is wicked. Most of them are good at their work." He was quiet a moment. "A conspiracy can be exposed. A convenience cannot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:209:"I've thought about it every night this week, who you'd file it with. The seat runs on it, and so does the registry, and every evaluation office in the Compact sends its readings through it. You'd be filing a complaint about the corrections function with an office whose whole business is entering what it advises, and they'd find nothing irregular, because every form's been filled in properly for two hundred years." She took her hand off the notebook. "There's nobody to send it to who isn't standing on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:21:There would be a certificate in it for every enrollee on the hill, four hundred and thirty of them. There would be one for Lira, from wherever she had been read, and one for Brom from the Velmere district, and one for Karis. There was no certificate in it for him, because the plate at Denvash had lit with a word and no station had ever written him down since. Every other paper in that drawer was three lines in a clerk's hand, and some of the three lines were what a station had read, and some of them were what an order had told it to write. There was no way on earth, standing at a counter in a queue, to tell which.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:29:"Hesk-ward," said the desk clerk. "Your sheet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:31:He took his sheet. Under the Fifth-day, in Seln's hand, it still said *excused (ribs)*. In wing three that morning the band bouts would go on without him, three cards and a figure, the Ash instructor and the Mire and Gault, and somewhere in the residence Ephram would be ruling his chart and leaving a second gap. Cael folded the sheet and put it in his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:41:He went to the old frames all the same, after the noon meal, because Ephram was there. Cael went with him and stood at the wall and watched. Brom did not lift anything. He stood beside the frames with his arms folded, the left one carefully, and watched Ephram work through the long frame's sets one after another without a word, the way they had stood together at those frames all year. Ephram did not ask about the shoulder. At the end of his sets he wiped his hands, and looked at Brom's left arm for one second, and nodded at it as if it had passed some test he had set it privately, and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:231:Two lanterns. The painted crosses and arrows going away across the planed floor into the half-dark. The frames along the east wall on their heavy springs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:311:Cael stood in the middle of the painted floor and looked at Brom's hand on the edge of the door, the big square hand that had held twelve feet of road against two men on the Ostrand road and held a girl up in a doorway for twelve seconds. Something in him went quiet that had nothing to do with the new thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:341:She put the two flames out with a closing of her hand, and stood, and moved the line. She drew it again two feet further from him, and lit its ends again, and counted three and four, and the new thing asked, and he named it. She moved it two feet further and counted five and six, and it asked a little less surely, as a voice is less sure across a wider room. At ten feet he named it, and Karis wrote *10* in her notebook and drew a ring round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:343:At twelve feet she lit the seventh and the eighth, and the asking was very faint, like somebody speaking in the next room with the door shut. At fourteen she drew the line and did not light it, and asked him, and there was nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:351:She went back to the line at twelve feet, to put out the last two flames, and it happened then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:353:The chalk line at twelve feet lay on the boards between its two small fires, and the new thing, which had been faint about it, was suddenly not faint. It leaned. It leaned the way it had leaned in his own doorway with Lira coming through it on her crutch, when he had been halfway through saying *no*. Something in him began to go out of him toward the line, quick and quiet as a breath, to set a point at one end of it and a point at the other and tell the space between them to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:7:*Fifth-day. Ninth sitting. Forty minutes by the exchange's clock. The house's counsel on the remedy: that no seat has ever before ordered a practitioner into a frame over a provision that stands. Jent: that the code allows it, and the petition asks for nothing the code doesn't give. Both true. The hood wrote for some time and said nothing. We came home.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:19:*Sixth-day. The honest floor, the four of us, after the tenth bell. Perception only. Ten feet, still, and faint at twelve. It didn't ask about the door with Brom's hand on it. It doesn't ask about much now. I named the lanterns out of habit and it had nothing to say about them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:31:The house's counsel came up the hill the night before the eleventh sitting, in her hired chaise, and sat in the reading room until the ninth bell with Karis and the code and a single sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:63:"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward," said the seat's clerk. "Upon the merits. The eleventh sitting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:69:"The house moves," she said, "under the provision of the code headed *Of the seat's hearing, and who may be heard*, in its second paragraph. The seat will find that heading struck through in the general index. It will find the provision itself standing in the text, unamended and unrepealed, in the same words in which it was enacted." She laid one sheet on the edge of the house's table for the clerk. "The first paragraph was invoked before this seat at the fourth sitting, without objection, and the advocate of record for the respondent was entered under it. The second provides that the advocate of record may be heard at the close, by the seat's leave. The house asks that leave. A note upon the provision's history is annexed. It is the work of an enrollee of the house's second year, who found it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:79:Jent stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:125:It was a plain room, like all the rooms on that corridor, with a bed and a press and a table under the window, and it was tidier than Cael's because Brom was. The two boxes were under the bed, roped, where they had been since the week of the gate, and Cael had stopped seeing them. The plaque, dark wood and a brass plate with the bar and ring, lay on the shelf on its face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:143:He had never asked. None of them had. It had been agreed in the recess before the year opened, in about as many words as it takes to pass the salt, that what came to Brom from Velmere was Brom's, and that nobody would ask what it offered or what he meant to answer. He had seen Brom's face, once, on the evening the first of this year's letters was opened, and had gone on not asking. He had watched that hand go to that pocket a dozen times a day through a road and a cache and a recess. He did not know one word of what was in either envelope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:15:He told the circle chalked on the worn boards of a district office, and the warden-adjunct three feet away watching the brass plate let into her desk, and the thing that woke behind his breastbone and came up to the edge of his sight. He told the eleven seconds he had counted while the plate did not light, and the one word that came at the end of them, very far away and very close, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick and never coming back when he looked. He told the letter that had made Denvash impossible for Hesk's sake, and the road east. He told Lira next, because Lira had come next: six weeks of bouts in a yard at Ardenmere, with her knocking him down on the boards between them more times than either of them had counted. Then one cold night on a cot under a window, with the Log on his knee, something had been written in the dark place behind his breastbone where the light had gone out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:91:"The bills show," said Cael. "The hip, the shoulder. People see a fighter with a bruise and a stiff walk, and they think bruise. Nobody's ever looked at a bruise and thought anything else." He thought of Ephram's three close-ruled pages. "The band shows, and Ephram's charted it for a year, but the band's the thing I chose to show. It breathes because we made it breathe."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:99:"You may say it to me twice more in your life," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:103:"What you have just told me, all of it, in order. Twice more, if there is ever a need, and you will know if there is, but not a third time." He said it as he would have said a rule of the wing to a first-year who had asked about floor hours. "Every telling is a copy, and a thing told three times is a thing on its way to being known. I have kept the case twelve years by never once describing it to anybody, and you will keep this by telling it as seldom as you can bear to." He paused. "I am not ungrateful. I am only telling you the price, because nobody told me mine until I had paid it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:105:"Twice more," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:107:"Twice more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:178:Cael did not watch his face. Two years across a counter had taught him that it was the last place on the man where anything would show. He watched the hands instead, flat on the green cloth, and saw the fingertips pressed down until they had gone pale, the way a clerk presses a ledger open at a page he means to read back through from the top. Cael knew which pages. The counter, the bench and the cup. The slips on the common-room fire. A season's figures that never once settled into a line. An afternoon at Norhold where everything held back went out in one breath, except one thing. Somewhere in all that, a boy had been holding a coat over him and never saying so, and the hands were finding each place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:182:"You kept my file too, then."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:262:*Told the whole of it tonight, in order, to the one person at that table who had never heard it, the first time from the beginning. It's longer than I thought, and shorter. Twice more in my life, he says, and not a third.*

