/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n 'ten counts|twelve counts|clean at|bill, not flag|lane|air|body|wrong thing|right thing|flag' editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-{01..13}.md | head -n 160; rg -n 'fixed point|fixed-point|point at|line between|cage|unbinding|unbound|doorway|twelve seconds|twelve-second|perception|ten feet|twelve feet|fourteen feet' editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-{28..47}.md | head -n 220" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:5:Nobody had announced the trial beyond a line on Rooke's own board, chalked the evening before in his square capitals: *PROGRAM TRIAL. SECOND-DAY, SECOND BELL. LIRA / FORCE THIRD-YEAR. RATED.* That was all. But the house had read it, and the house knew what *rated* meant on that floor. It meant the first bout ever fought on the program's painted boards under the program's own seal, with a figure at the end of it that would go into the program's own book. By the half-bell the gallery was full along both sides. By the second bell the porters had opened the shutters on the high windows to let the heat out, and the light came down onto the white crosses in long bright bars, and there were people standing on the gallery stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:19:"Anything. His stamps. His settles. How long he laughs. You'll see things from the rail I won't see from the floor, and I won't be able to ask you about them till it's over, and I want them all, in order, when I do." She finished the wrist and flexed the hand, and looked at it as if it belonged to somebody she was fond of. "Karis will count my bursts whether I like it or not. That's her business. Yours is him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:21:Down the walk the Force came round the corner of the range with two of his cohort, all three of them laughing at something, and saw her on the step, and lifted a hand. She lifted one back. Then she stood, and went in to the floor without another word, and Cael went up the gallery stair to find the rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:23:Cael had the rail at the north end, where he had stood on the second morning of the year to watch the red book. Brom stood on his left with his arms folded. Karis stood on his right with her notebook shut and her pen in her hand. Ephram was three steps down the gallery stair with the captain's file under his arm, where he could see the whole floor and be seen by the whole cohort, which Cael supposed was the point. The girl from the reading room's outer table had found a place against the far wall of the gallery, with her small notebook open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:25:Below them on the floor stood the rating table: a trestle under the north windows with three chairs behind it. The clerk with the red drill-book sat at one end. Rooke sat in the middle with his board and his pencil. And at the other end, with a ruled card in front of him and his hands folded on the table like a man waiting outside a shop, sat the Stone from the river academy, who had asked to come and teach the girl who beat him and was now going to put a figure on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:29:"Program trial. My rule, not the region's." He held up his board as if the rule were written on it, which it was not. "Four exchanges. Each one, the table gives a flag to whoever took it, or to nobody if nobody did. Most flags takes the trial. Three bursts a bout for the program's entrant, as on every day she's ever stood on this floor. Past three, the table still gives the exchange to whoever takes it, and the card pays for every burst over. The other fighter, what his own Path allows. Level after four, the table rates them both, and the higher figure takes it." He sat down. "The figure goes in this program's book. Not the registry's. Not yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:35:He was a big young man, broad in the back and thick through the thighs, and he stood at his chalk with his feet planted and his hands loose and a pleasant expression, the expression of somebody who has been looking forward to a thing and has decided to enjoy it. He had been drilling beside Lira every morning since the recess. He knew the four-count; he had run it himself, badly, for the practice of seeing it. He had laughed once on this floor when she lied to him on the second count and he went the wrong way. He was Iron Rank Seven, six ranks above her on the porter's wall, and he was the best thing the cohort had built in a straight line since Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:39:From the rail there was nothing else to watch. A Force fighter's shove went on contact, and this one's contact was the floor. He stamped with his front foot, and a beat later the boards ahead of the stamp, out to a stride, jumped, as a table jumps when somebody slaps it from beneath, and went on jumping for a count or two before they lay down. Then the shove came back up into him along the line it had gone out by, and his knees gave and his hips dropped a finger's breadth while he took it. That was the settle. Cael could see it from the rail and hear the boards answer it. It was the bill for the last stamp, paid before the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:51:The Stone lifted his flag. Red. The Force's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:71:Karis, on his other side, had opened her notebook. She wrote one line and showed it to him without a word. *First flag bought. Price: one flag on the record, in front of the table.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:77:The first step was an ordinary step, any fighter's, and the Force watched it come and stamped again. The second step was the lie. She put her weight on her front foot and left it there one count too long, the long lie the Stone had taught her, so that anybody watching for the burst would see it coming and set himself to meet it. The Force saw it coming. He stamped exactly where her burst would land if it came off that front foot, a good hard stamp, and Cael heard the boards jump there a beat later and thought it was the best the man had done all morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:81:It was the hardest thing in the world to watch. Lira stood on the painted floor with one foot forward and one back and did nothing at all, for a whole count, while a Rank Seven Force stood twenty feet away with the boards still jumping in front of him and his knees bent, waiting. The gallery made a small sound. Somebody laughed, uncertainly. The Force laughed too, from surprise, the way he always did, a short bark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:85:Cael saw it. Lira saw it a hair before he did. The settle was on the man's own fourth count, his own rhythm, the same rhythm he had paid to in the first exchange with nothing in his way. He had to take his own shove back, whether she had given him anything to shove at or not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:93:The Stone lifted his flag. Blue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:97:"Two bursts," said Karis, writing. "Two exchanges. One flag each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:117:She had done it on the honest floor three nights before, at the end of a lane he laid for her, with Brom standing at the end of the line. She had learned it standing in dust that ran. *Lie with the foot you don't care about. Go on the fourth.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:159:"Three flags to one," said the clerk into the noise, and wrote it in the red book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:161:The Stone wrote on his card. He wrote for a long time, longer than a single figure needed. Then he slid the card along the trestle to Rooke, and Rooke read it, and stood up with it in his hand, and the gallery fell quiet to hear it, because nobody on the hill had ever heard a program figure read out on that floor before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:185:Ephram came down the gallery stair against the stream of people going up it to leave, with the captain's file under his arm and a pencil behind his ear, and stopped beside Cael at the rail's end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:187:"I've started her a page," he said. He opened the file a little way, enough to show a sheet folded in four inside it, new, with only three lines on it so far. "I keep one on everybody. You know that. Hers was a line a month. It's going to be longer now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:193:He went off toward the south door to tell the cohort where to stand, and Cael heard his voice go up behind him in the yard, patient and carrying, explaining to somebody that a captain did not have to know why a thing had happened in order to know that it had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:195:On his way to the south door the Force stopped at the foot of the gallery stair, kit on his shoulder, wraps hanging out of his coat pocket. Lira was on the bottom step by then, unwinding her tapes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:207:He nodded, a short honest nod, the way a man nods to somebody who has told him where he left his hat, and went out of the south door. Cael watched him go and knew that by Rooke's next morning drill the man would be stamping to a rhythm nobody on the hill could hear, and that the whole cohort would be the better for having had him beaten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:209:Lira came up the stair to the rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:215:Nobody said anything. Brom unfolded his arms. Karis, on Cael's other side, closed her notebook very quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:217:Lira looked at the floor a moment longer, and then went down the stair to find her water, walking carefully on the left leg, and did not look back to see how it had been received.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:225:He stopped on the flags and wrote it there: the date, and the one word under Lira's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:237:The first came on the thirty-first day, to Bracken's counter, in the district seat's grey stock with the district's own small seal, and Bracken carried it across to the refectory himself at the noon meal. He did not lay it on the table this time. He stood at the end of the middle table and read it aloud, all of it, which he had never done with anybody's paper in that room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:243:"You've read more dockets than anybody at this table but me," he said. "Have you ever seen that on one?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:247:"Nor have I, and every docket that came up this hill in thirteen years has crossed my counter." He took the sheet back. "A docket gives the day and the room and the panel. Nobody lets it guess at the end. Some district clerk got tired of guessing quietly and wrote his guess where we could all see it." He looked at the word over his spectacles as if it might go away. "I'd like to shake his hand. I'd also like to have him moved. I'll be writing to the district." He held the sheet out across the table to Brom. "Well. They've as good as painted it on the door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:255:The second envelope came up with the evening post. Cael saw it on the post-room shelf when he went for his own letters, a square cream envelope with a dark red seal pressed with the bar and the ring, and he knew the seal. Brom took it from the shelf at the eighth bell. He did not open it in the post room or the court. He took it upstairs and shut his door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:259:Lira was sitting on the stair in the dark when he came in, with her bad leg out straight along the step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:265:"His door doesn't latch unless you lean on it." She looked at her own hands. "I didn't lean on it. I wanted to." She was quiet a moment. "When he opens it, nobody asks. Not what it said. Not whether he's all right. Tell Karis. I'll tell her too, so she hears it twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:267:Karis heard it twice. She did not say anything about it to either of them. But from the next evening on, the annex door stood open every night until the last bell, with the lamp lit inside and Karis at the long table with her back to the stair, and anybody coming up could have walked in and sat down without knocking. Nobody remarked on it. Brom went past it twice the next evening on his way to the stair and did not go in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:271:Nobody saw him do it. Nobody at the table learned what it said, because he did not tell them, then or afterward. What they saw was his face, when he came down to supper an hour later than usual and sat in his place and pulled the bread toward him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:277:Nobody asked. Lira passed Brom the beans. Brom took some, and passed them on to Seln, who took some, and passed them on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:5:For two months the bluff had been nearly his. There had been the porters and the kitchen and the fire-watch boy, and Bracken in the records hall with his door open, and the four of them and a handful of others who had nowhere better to be. Now the wagons had been coming up the road since noon. Doors banged along the residence front. Somebody below was singing the same three lines of a song over and over, getting the third line wrong each time. The fountain in the court ran on under all of it, as steady as ever, but it was no longer the loudest thing on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:15:*The house: full again tonight. Somebody downstairs can't learn a song.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:23:*Seln: at the copying table every working day of the recess, and at the middle of the bench every evening. Nobody has said a word about the bench. Nobody needs to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:27:He stopped there and looked down into the court for a while. A first-year was dragging a trunk across the flags on its corner, too proud to ask for help. Then he went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:33:*What I carry, at the year's open: seven confirmed, the seventh Daeva's storm, and the quiet thing from the stair among them, sealed. Under all seven, the still place I don't count. The storm still has its flag on it. Nine of ten, this last week, the Wind came when I called and nothing else came instead. Nine isn't ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:43:There was a pull in him somewhere, faint, like a coat caught on a nail as you go through a door. He had felt it every morning since Norhold. Some mornings it was in the hip and some mornings in the chest, and some mornings he could not have said where it was at all. He lay and listened to the house breathe for a while. Then he got up and dressed in the dark and went down the residence stair in his stockings, carrying his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:45:The second floor stood under the middle roof, across the court and up one flight, and at that hour it was black. He lit the lantern by the door from the porter's tinderbox and turned it low. The boards ran away from him into the dark, plain pine, a little stiffer than the sprung oak of the Crown yard and a great deal less forgiving, laid long ago by somebody who must have believed that the best way to teach a fighter about falling was to make him do it on something hard. On oak he had six free bursts in a day. Here it was five, and he had stopped being surprised by that a long time ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:73:And it went nowhere. The blow came into the forearm and stopped there, all of it, as if he had caught it in a pocket. Nothing went back up the arm into the shoulder. Nothing went down into the hip. The bag hung against his arm for a heartbeat as if it were puzzled, and then swung away on its chain with the dull creak of a gate. His forearm hummed, deep in the bone, and the hum faded slowly. That was the bill, and it was small. It had always been small, until somebody hit it too hard or too often.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:83:The quiet thing from the stair he did not try. He never tried it; it was not a thing that needed calling. It came by itself in the gaps of the day whether he called it or not, and he had paid rent on it every day since he took it, from the moment he woke to the moment he slept, spent or unspent. It was sealed. It would stay sealed until the debt it was sealed for was spoken, and nobody had spoken it yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:89:It had come to him in the fourth exchange of the hardest bout of his life, in front of eight thousand people, out of the hands of a Gold who had begun the fight herself, and who had looked at him afterward on the stone as if he had asked her a question nobody had ever asked her before. The notice that came after it was the first that had ever said *Gold*. It was also the first that had ever warned him that what it gave him might not hold. *Stability: provisional — architecture under load.* The notice had found him on one knee on the stone, with a healer's fingers in his hand and eight thousand people looking down, and he had read it through twice there, before anybody strapped him. He had felt its words every day since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:91:Gold was heavy. Everything else he carried had been shifting to make room for it, slowly, the way the furniture in a house shifts when somebody carries a great iron chest in through the front door. For weeks after Norhold he had reached for the Wind and got something else. Some mornings it was the push, and his whole body shoved at nothing. Once, on the road, it was the read, and he had stood in a waystation yard knowing the exact weight of the water butt and going nowhere. The healers had said a month. His own count had said more, and his own count had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:95:The lantern by the door gave the boards a little light, and beyond the light they ran into the dark. There was dust on them, the fine pale dust of a floor nobody had swept since the recess began. He looked at the dust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:97:Then he asked the air to lean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:99:He had never found better words for it. Daeva had her own words, and they were not his. To him it was like putting a hand flat against the morning and pressing, and feeling the whole of it give a little, along one line, the way a board gives when you step on its end. The air in front of him tipped. Fifteen feet of it, no more, in a narrow road no wider than his shoulders, and it reached perhaps a third as deep as hers had reached. His ears closed and opened, and the dust on the boards woke up. It ran, in a thin pale stream along the floor, down the length of the lane, as water runs down a gutter, and gathered against the far edge of it in a little drift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:103:Fifteen feet. Then the end of the lane, like the end of a moving walkway at a fair, and he was his own weight again and stopping. Behind him the air let go, and his ears closed and opened once more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:107:This was the moment it always came. After the lane, every other thing he carried had pulled toward the place the lane had been, like iron filings toward a lodestone, and for a minute or two nothing answered true. He waited for it. He waited for the coat to catch on the nail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:125:No notice came, and none ever would. Notices came when something began; nothing had ever come to tell him a thing had finished. He had known that, and he found he had half expected one anyway, as a boy half expects a letter on the morning of a fair. The floor was quiet, and the lantern had burned down a little. Somewhere outside a cockerel was arguing with the light, and the light was winning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:127:He knew then, sitting on the boards, that he had been afraid. Not of the bout, and not of the notice, and not of Gold. He had been afraid, for eleven weeks, that the house in him would never sit true again, and that he would spend the rest of his life reaching for one thing and getting another, on floors where getting the wrong thing would cost somebody else as well as him. He had not let himself write it down, because writing it down would have made it a finding. He let himself feel it now, all of it, because it was over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:135:*First-day, before light. The second floor. The full suite in order. Wind, five of five; the read, clean; the push, four, flat at the last; the give, the bag twice; the spark, one contact on the porters' basin; the lane, fifteen feet, a third deep, the dust ran. Then the Wind again, after the lane. It came alone.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:139:*Eleven weeks. The flag is down. Settled.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:149:The minute from the bluff was in the front of this book, in his best hand, three lines long: nothing made to happen; only inside a fight somebody else begins; the cost on paper first. And under it, in Karis's wording, with four sets of initials, the line they had added at Norhold after the bill came in. *Never again in the middle of a fight unless what follows has been priced first, at the weight it will come at.* At Norhold nobody had known the weight. Now he knew it. Fifteen feet, a third deep, the ears, the dust. Eleven weeks of everything leaning. That was what Gold cost, on paper, at last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:157:He did not feel it begin. He never did. One moment he was a boy sitting on a floor in the grey light. The next he was a little less than that: not gone, only thinner, a place the eye would slide over on its way to somewhere more interesting. He knew it only by the absence it left, like a stair that is not there in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:161:He did what he had trained himself to do in every gap of every day since the stair. He gave a corner of his attention to somebody. There was nobody on the floor, so he went to the window. Below, under the covered walk's north arch, the fire-watch boy was standing on his upturned bucket with his slate, chalking the night's last hour. He had his tongue between his teeth. He made the figure, and looked at it, and rubbed it out with his cuff, and made it again more carefully.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:163:Watching the boy was not hiding. A person who was awake to one other person was visible to everybody. The two halves of the thing in him would not run together; he could not be the most watchful creature on the hill and the least noticed at the same time. Not yet. The thinness went out of him like breath from a cold window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:173:He sat where he had sat for two years, fourth row, north side, with the pillar at his shoulder. From there he could see the whole faculty bench and only half of the platform, and half of the platform was all anybody needed. He counted the hall as it filled. It was the first thing a room would tell you without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:177:They went to him first. He had come in alone before the bell and taken his pillar, and he had felt them find him one after another, like a hand laid on the back of his neck. Then Karis came through the far door with her grey notebook already open, and a scatter of heads moved after her, the few who had paid to read transcripts. When Brom sat down at the row's far end, a bench of Stone first-years leaned forward together, as if a weight had been set on the bench in front of them. And Lira came last, late from the east hall with her hair still wet, and half the intake turned in its seats to watch her walk the length of the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:185:"Two of the second-years came to see me last night." Ephram undid the tape. "They have a list. Every meal of the term, two names against it, and a table chosen from which the pair of them can study how you hold a spoon. They wanted it countersigned by the captain, as a cohort activity."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:199:The house's ordinary business took her about as long as it takes to tie a pair of boots, and nobody remembered most of it afterward: rooms, hours, a new instructor for the Current cohort, a wall on the north side that wanted pointing and was to be kept away from until it had been. Then, in the same voice and at the same speed, she read out the year's appointments, and the third of them was a floor. Coach Rooke's Silver-bracket program, which the house had carried through the recess on credit, had its own charter from this morning, its own hall and its own line in the accounts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:201:The third-years' benches understood it first, and the noise began there and spread backward into the intake's block before most of the intake knew what it was cheering. Somebody near the back stamped. Then a great many people were stamping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:209:"We went to Norhold," said Withrow, "and came home third, and you have all heard me say so, and you will not hear me say it again." She let a breath go by. "Here is the year instead. It is quieter work and it is harder work. Third is a figure now, written down in other people's books, and a figure in other people's books is a thing other people may try to rub out. There is one way to keep it, and it is the plain way. Say what you are. Be it. Leave it where anyone can check." Her eyes went nowhere in particular, which with Withrow was always somewhere. "Somebody will be checking. Somebody always is, now. Good. The research standings post on the covered walk this morning. The program's floor hours post at noon. Go to your assignments."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:233:He was at the east end of the hall, by the tall windows, alone, standing with his hands clasped behind his back and his head turned a little to one side, the way a man stands in a doorway at night when he thinks he has heard a step on the stair and is waiting to hear it again. The east windows looked down the bluff road as far as the bend above the trees, and anybody who showed at that bend still had two switchbacks to climb before the lodge-man heard a hoof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:235:There was nobody on the road. Cael could see that much from here, through the glass, over the heads of the last people going out. The road lay pale and empty in the early heat, and the dust on it had not been raised since the last of the wagons.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:247:"Yes." She considered the registrar's back. "And he hasn't told it to anybody, which means it's either not his to tell or not ready to be told." She turned toward the door. "I'll be in my room. I've a lock to get used to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:11:She was asleep when he came in. She was sitting upright in her chair at the long table with her arms folded on the wood in front of her and her head on her arms, and the pen had rolled out of her hand and stopped against the inkwell. The lamp at her elbow had burned itself dry and gone out. In front of her, squared into one stack so neat that it might have been cut with a blade, lay the night's work in her finding hand. The girl at the outer table across the landing had put a shawl over Karis's shoulders and then, apparently, not dared to do anything else. She sat at her own table with her hands in her lap, guarding the door from inside the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:23:"Ninety-one." She sat back and the shawl slid off her shoulders, and she did not notice it go. "Every time anybody has ever called on the integrity provisions since the registry began writing it down. I've read every one. I found them in the registry digests. Bracken's press has the oldest digests and the hall has the rest, and Seln's sheaf told me which year each section first appears in, which saved me a week." She laid her hand on the stack. "Eighty-four of them are about things. Frames. Stations. An assessor who took money in a district I won't name. Two panels whose minutes argued with themselves. A records office that lost a whole quarter's declarations in a flood and then tried to pretend it hadn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:27:"Seven are about people." She looked at him. "Every one is about something a person did. A false declaration. A bought result. An evaluator who sat on his own nephew's panel. Seven times in the registry's whole history, somebody has pointed these provisions at a human being. Not once was it about what the human being was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:33:"The ninety-second. And the first of its kind." She moved the stack an inch toward him. "Read it. Then come down to the counter with me. There's somebody I want to show something to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:39:*Ostrand district station; sigil frame reading Stone practitioners one rank above their true rank over one month; integrity provisions, section four; filed by the registrar of Halcenvane in his fourth year in office; frame withdrawn, tested a fortnight, a seam in the casing found admitting damp, repaired, the month's Stones re-read; sustained.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:63:Cael understood, walking up the library stair behind her afterward, that he had just watched two people who did not say kind things to each other say one. Karis had put Bracken's own filing into the answer, as evidence that the integrity provisions were for frames; it was the strongest single line on the leaf, because it had been filed by a registrar at the very counter that was answering. And Bracken had asked for his own small error to be left standing in it, so that nobody could ever say the house had tidied the record to suit itself. Neither of them had said a word about why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:77:"You can't prove *never*," said the counsel. "You can prove ninety-one. A digest can miss a filing. A district can have burned its records. A seat with a mind to be awkward will ask you how you know about the ones nobody wrote down." She lifted the rule. "Say *not once in the registry's record*. Then it's a count, and it's yours, and nobody can argue with it but a man with a ninety-second filing in his hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:87:"That's all there is." The counsel stood and buttoned her coat. "I've read a good many answers to a good many petitions. That's the best section on these provisions I've seen written by anybody, and I include my own. Put it in." She picked up her bag. "I'll be back up the hill when the rest of it's done. Send for me early. I like to read things twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:89:When she had gone down the stair, Karis did not say anything for a while. She sat with her hands folded on the forty pages. Then she tore a strip off the bottom of a used sheet and wrote two lines on it, fast, and pushed it across the table to him without looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:101:The queue had shortened since the first days. The intake had learned the counter's hours and stopped coming in a body. A single Ash first-year stood ahead of him with a form she had filled in twice, once in pencil and once over the pencil in ink. Behind the counter the desk clerk added his column by the window. Gault's door stood shut. And at the copying table, under the shelf with the small unmarked case on it, the teaching assistant rose to take the first-year's sheet and read it from the name to the signature at his even pace, and stamped it, and tore the slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:125:He had thought, for two years, that Seln did not give things. He saw now that this was wrong. Seln gave things all the time. He gave forms before anybody had asked for them, and a sheaf under a door before dawn, and a correction about an index to a boy at a counter who could carry it upstairs. He gave them in a way that made thanks impossible, because there was no record of the thing and so nothing to thank him for. It was not modesty. Cael did not think it was even kindness, exactly. It was a man who had spent his life on the side of a counter where everything that passed across it was written down, making very sure that the one thing he chose to give stayed off the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:127:He thought about the quiet thing he carried, sealed, and the debt it was sealed for, which the man at the copying table did not know he was owed. He thought that it would be a very hard debt to pay to somebody who would not let anything be paid to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:129:He went up the library stair and told Karis about the index. She went straight to the digests without a word. Twenty minutes later she found that one of her ninety-one, a coastal panel that had sat a member short, carried a year she had taken from the second volume's index, and the index had it a dozen years late. She ruled the year through and wrote the right one above it. It changed nothing in the argument. It went in anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:159:In the morning the court steamed. Bracken sent the porter's boy up the library stair with a message for Karis, and the message was one word long, and Karis read it on the landing and came down at once with the boy still trying to keep up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:163:They weighed it in the post room behind the counter, on the brass beam the house used for parcels going down the hill, where letters were weighed for their postage and nobody had ever weighed anything that mattered. The answer had been bound in two parts, because the binder's thread would not go through it in one, and the two parts lay side by side in a leather document wallet with the house's seal on the flap. Bracken set the wallet on the pan himself. He put on the brass weights one at a time from the box, the big ones first, and then the small, and then the very small ones that lived in their own velvet slots, until the beam came level and stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:169:"Every answer the house has filed in my time," said Bracken, to nobody, and put it away. "I like to know what they weighed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:191:Cael watched from the covered walk with half the house beside him. Nobody had announced anything. Nobody needed to. The head groom came out of the stable yard on the bigger of the two greys in the coat he wore at convocation and nowhere else, and his son on the other grey looking as if he had been told to sit up straight and was doing it with his whole body. Between them rode Bracken's senior clerk, a long patient man who had kept the enrollment book for nine years and had never in his life been looked at by so many people at once. The wallet was strapped across the front of his saddle under a fold of the house's own cloth, and he kept one hand flat on it the whole way across the court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:195:The lodge-man stood as straight as he stood for the Chancellor. The intake crowded the north wall to watch them take the first switchback, and the second, and the bend above the trees. Lira was on the wall with them, and Brom, and somewhere behind Cael on the walk a third-year said, half laughing and half not, *Is that it? Is that the whole answer?* and somebody else said, *That's eleven pounds of it,* and nobody laughed after that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:199:Karis did not come down to watch. Cael looked up at the top of the library, at the reading room's window, and she was not in it. She had gone to sleep in her chair the moment the wallet left the counter, the girl at the outer table told him afterward, with her boots still on and her key on its cord in her fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:221:He started at the end, with the morning after, because that was where a person actually lived. The ferryman with a letter, the guild hall with a letter, the clerk with a sealed file and no key. A daughter at Withrow's counter every week for a year, asking whether anybody had begun the review, and nobody ever obliged to begin it. Then the three steps of chapter fourteen in Bracken's voice that would not let itself be a voice, and the third word that nobody had ever had to stamp. Then the six slips of paper laid out down the long table like stones across a stream, each a door, each real law, each opening toward the next and never back the way you had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:235:"In the examination hall. Three examiners at a table, and a slate in a wooden frame between them." She held her hands a foot apart. "They chalked it while I fought. Afterward the one in the middle copied it fair onto the form, and wiped the slate with his sleeve so the form would have no crossings-out. I remember thinking how careful that was." She put her hands down. "Every line on that form was correct. Wind. Copper. My name spelt right. Every step in the book, in the book's order. It was the room that was wrong. I've never been allowed to say both of those at once and have anybody listen. I only had *angry*, and angry's a very poor word. It's all one size."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:243:"Here's the other half," said Lira. "There was no door out of that hall but the one they pointed at. I went out of it with the form in my pocket, and for a year after that I knocked on guild doors. I asked six of them for a letter and walked to two. One wouldn't let me in the door. The other let me into the hall, and read the form, and was very polite." She turned her head toward him. "Then I came to Ardenmere with nothing left to knock on, and one evening I heard a noise like somebody hitting a boiler with a chain, three streets off, and went to look. Vell's yard. Nobody sent me there. It wasn't on any form. It was round the back of everything, where the town kept what it didn't want to look at, and that turned out to include me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:247:"So I believe Karis," she said. "Every door opens one way. I've stood in that building. But there's usually a yard out the back." She looked down at him. "Not that anybody draws it on the plan."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:251:He wrote one line before the light went: *Lira: a slate wiped clean, a year of polite doors, then a yard nobody drew.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:5:The light had come for it in the end. It lay across the first leaf in a long pale bar, and the fountain was running in the court below, and somewhere along the residence front a door banged and a voice called for somebody's other boot. The ordinary morning of an ordinary house. He lay a while and listened to it, and counted what he could hear, and found that the counting did not help.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:11:Karis was not at breakfast. Her chair at the end of the table stood pushed in square, and her cup was gone from the rack, which meant she had been down before the bell and taken it up with her. Lira sat across from his place with her elbows either side of a bowl of porridge she was not eating. Brom ate for two, steadily, as if somebody had told him he would need it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:13:"She's been up there since the third bell," said Lira. "Brom heard her on the stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:25:They had the door of the old lumber room propped open with a broken chair, and they were working the table through it on its side, sweating and grumbling at it in the low courteous voices porters used on the library stair. Behind them the room itself stood half empty. It had held, for as long as Cael had known it, the house's spare benches and a map case with no maps in it and two rolled carpets nobody would admit to owning. Now the carpets were out on the landing and the benches gone, and somebody had already swept the floor in long straight strokes and left the broom against the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:27:"Registrar's orders," said the older porter, when Cael held the door for them. "Table, two chairs, a shelf for big books. He says big books." He looked at the shelf, which was very big. "He says a girl's to sit in here and nobody's to go past her who hasn't been asked for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:31:"Second-year. Ink on her." The porter set his end of the table down. "She's already been and moved the chairs twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:33:By supper the house would have a name for the two rooms together, the reading room and the cleared one facing it across the landing. Ephram would use it first, to a knot of curious first-years at the foot of the library stair, and it would stick: the annex.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:41:"It was under the door when I came up," said Karis without looking round. "At the third bell. Pushed through flat, so it wouldn't crease." She turned a page of the code. "The porter's boy was sweeping this stair at the second bell. He says the wing's teaching assistant came up it with something under his arm, and went down it again without, and wished him good morning on the way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:51:Four days to the answer's bones, said Karis. After that, the flesh, and then Bracken and the house's counsel and the weighing, and then it went down the hill with time in hand. Every leaf of the code that left the records hall came back to it at night, because Bracken would not sleep with the press unlocked and she would not ask him to. Brom would carry. Brom had already been told and had already said yes, in one word, at the foot of the stair at the second bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:63:By the first bell after noon there was a pair of second-years on the records-hall steps, standing very straight. One was the Mire boy who looked at ceilings. The other was the girl from the short table with her small notebook, and she was writing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:69:"Of course she is. I told her to." Lira looked at the steps. "Somebody might as well."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:91:Nobody had asked him that about the name before. Not at Denvash, where Pellin had stamped it on his card; not at Greyvane, where it had been read out over his head; not on any form since. Once, at Ardenmere, he had asked Vell to put it in her book the right way round, which was the nearest he had come. The registry gave him the name the way it gave a ledger a number. People said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:105:The first was Karis's own finding from Greyvane, the long one, the one she had built from the standardization directive's repeal schedules a line at a time until she could say, flatly, in front of a seat, that nobody had ever repealed the clause. It had been left behind, like a child at a fair, when everything round it was swept up and carried off. The second was the house counsel's reconstruction, made the year after, by another road, through the registry's own index of surviving provisions, without reading a word of Karis's first. The third was Prynn's copy and the note on its cover, from the archive at Greyvane, which showed the clause standing in the founding text with nothing written against it in two hundred years of hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:119:At noon Brom came up the library stair with the second volume of the code on his shoulder, the way a miller's man carries a sack, and set it down on the shelf in the lumber room without a sound. The girl at the outer table stood up when he came in. She sat down again when he went out. Then she got up and moved the volume an inch along the shelf so that its spine stood level with the others, and sat down once more and went on writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He read it at his own window that night, with the fountain going in the court and the copy of the instrument shut in its folder on the bed behind him. Across the top of the grid ran the columns: the sitting, the panel, the day, the finding, the seal. Down the side ran the three times his place on the bluff had been written down by somebody obliged to write it. The grant at Greyvane, from the hearing. The first renewal, at Halcenvane, in the inspection year, before the Compact's delegation. The renewal at the mill town, last year, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:He remembered the room by its east wall, because that was where the strangers had been. Four chairs set out by Bracken at a guest's distance from one another. The delegation's counsel in the nearest, untying a folder's ribbon with her teeth. Ilsev beside her, sharpening a pencil into a fold of paper on her knee. Havel with a board across his knees, writing the date. And in the fourth chair the Archmarshal, with nothing in his hands at all. Three hours of instruments on the oak under those four chairs: the frames, the board, the pendulum ticking in its case. Then Gault reading his note aloud at the trestle, and the Ash instructor signing, and the Mire instructor adding her four lines about the faulty frame, and the clerk bringing the stamp down on the house's copy. *Renewed.* That had been the day, and the house's copy had been stamped on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:197:"There is no such thing as a small error in a document about a person." She blotted the new leaf and laid it in its place in the pile. "Nobody would have called it a lie. They'd only have called us careless, once, in one line. And after that, every leaf of ours the seat turned over, it would be turning over looking for the next one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:203:"I saw that two papers disagreed. I couldn't see why." She did not pretend otherwise. "I could have spent a morning in the inspection's papers. Or I could hand the table to the one of us who was in Gault's office that afternoon, and not say what I was looking for." She did look at him then. "If I'd told you there was a mistake on it, you'd have found one. You'd have found one on a clean leaf. I wanted to see what you'd find if you were only reading. Gault is a signature now. The Archmarshal is a chair. You're the one witness who can't be replaced, and witnesses see more when they don't know what they're being asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:209:And on the way down the stair he remembered the counter, and the four sentences Seln had spent on him in a queue on the third day of the year. *Standing papers on the provision come up for the ledger's half-year check. Keep the documentation current.* The renewals table was the provision's standing papers, set out in a grid. Whatever went down the hill this week would sit in the registry's file beside the registry's own dates when the half-year check came, and a table that disagreed with them by nine days would have been the first thing the check found. Karis had not heard Seln say it. She had kept the documentation current anyway, because that was what she did with documents.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:219:Not the burst, nor the exchange given away. Those were the band's ordinary prices, and he paid them every Fifth-day without looking at the bill. It was the hour. Up the library stair, Karis was spending a week of her life laying his name on a table one true day at a time, and he had spent this morning in front of three cards making sure the house's book held a slightly smaller version of him than the one she was writing down. The band had always been a daily expense. He had never before had to pay it in the same week as somebody else paid for the truth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:225:She came in late, when the refectory was half empty and the porters had begun stacking the benches at the far end. She had washed her hands and the ink had not come off. She sat down at the end of the middle table in her own chair, and Brom, without being asked, pushed the dish of beans down the board to her, and Seln, at the middle of the bench, moved his cup out of its way by exactly the width of the dish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:231:Nobody cheered. Lira put her elbows on the table and her chin on her fists and looked at her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:5:It was dark inside and smelled of new paint and old sweat. Brom had brought two lanterns and a jug of water, and he set the lanterns on the floor at either end of the painted lines and turned them up, and the white crosses and arrows came out of the dark across the boards like the bones of something. Lira went up the gallery stair two at a time and along the gallery, and Cael heard her drawing the shutters on the high windows one after another, all the way down the north wall, until there was no light in the hall but theirs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:9:"Karis says she's reading," she said. "She'll take the figures at breakfast. And if anybody breaks anything, she wants to know which thing, and she wants it in writing, in that order."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:13:"Which thing. She was very clear." Lira sat on the bottom step of the gallery stair to tape her wrists. "She says writing first is how you end up with a very neat page about the wrong bone." She tore a strip with her teeth. "And before you ask, I asked. Rooke lent me the floor after the tenth bell, any night I want it. He said he didn't want to know what for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:15:They had not done this since the road. On the road there had been no floor, and in the recess there had been the flag, and for eleven weeks nobody had wanted to see what the suite would do when it reached for one thing and got another. Lira had raised it at supper, on the evening of the fifth day, with her elbows on the table and her voice low. *It's settled. You wrote it settled. So show us.* Brom had said nothing, which from Brom was agreement, and Karis had said she would come if it were any other night of the week, and it was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:17:So here they were, three people and two lanterns on a floor somebody had painted for another purpose entirely, at an hour when the rest of the house was asleep or pretending to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:31:"Rules," said Lira from the stair. "Mine. I've been thinking about them all week, so you don't get to change them." She tore the tape with her teeth. "No band. No rotation. No rate. Whatever you've got, you use it, and whatever it costs, you tell us, and we write it down. Nobody's watching. Nobody's charting. If it goes off the top of a page, there isn't a page."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:53:Lira stopped dead on the boards. Her face did something he had not seen it do in a long time: it went entirely still, and then it lit from the inside, like a lantern somebody has turned up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:61:They ran it nine times in all, and he lost count of his own bursts somewhere about the seventh, because he had not been keeping them; that was the rule. She kept hers. Three in each bout, Rooke's rule, never a fourth, even here. *I'm not breaking Rooke's rule for you,* she said, when he asked. *I'd have to explain it to him, and he'd write one word on my sheet, and it would be the wrong word.* So she had three bursts and he had as many as he wanted, and she still took two of the nine off him. Both times she did it on the third count, the empty one, by doing nothing at all for one beat longer than he could believe anybody would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:71:"That was one bout." Lira wrote on the back of her hand with a stub of pencil. "This was nine. And tonight you weren't fighting somebody who wanted you to last. You were fighting me." She looked at what she had written. "I made you spend."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:75:She sat back on her heels and looked at the painted floor as if she had just been given it. Then she said, more quietly, "He was right. The Stone. He said the drill was only worth anything against somebody who could see it coming. Everybody here can't see it coming. You can." She stood. "I'm going to want to do this again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:85:Cael went down the floor to him and set his own feet and held up his left forearm across his body.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:115:This was the one Cael had shut his hand on in wing three. He set his right palm flat against Brom's chest, below the collarbone, and Brom set himself to it, the way he set himself against anything, with his weight down in his heels and his knees soft and his whole body arranged to be the thing that did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:127:Cael gave him four more. At the fourth his right shoulder spoke, as it always spoke at the fourth. At the fifth it said something else, longer and lower, and he knew he would be counting it on the stair in the morning. Brom slid each time, less each time, because each time he learned a little more about where to put his weight, and at the fifth he slid no more than a hand's breadth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:155:He asked the air to lean, along a line between them, from where he stood to the gap past Brom's left shoulder. Fifteen feet of it, a third deep, and the air tipped like a board stepped on at one end, and his ears closed and opened. Down at his feet the dust in the cracks of the boards woke up and ran, pale, along the lane toward Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:159:He felt it go. He had meant to keep the read on Brom's body, on the heels and the hips and the weight going down. Instead, the moment he laid the lane, the read went into the lane itself. It read the air: how hard it leaned, how far it ran, where it would let go. It was a beautiful read. It told him exactly where the end of the lane was. And it told him nothing at all about Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:161:He stepped into the lane and it carried him, fast, toward the gap past Brom's shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:163:Brom was not there. Brom had moved half a beat before, and the read had not seen it go, because the read was looking at the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:165:The forearm came across Cael's chest like a door shutting in a gale. He went down on the boards on his back, and the lane let go above him, and all the breath he owned went up into the dark of the gallery and stayed there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:175:The read could take a body or it could take the air. It could not take both at once. Not at Gold. Not yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:179:"Again," he said. "I'm going to lay it first, before I need it, and then give the read back to you. The lane doesn't need watching once it's down. You do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:183:The second time, he laid the lane while they were still walking to their marks. It ran from his feet to the gap past Brom's left shoulder, fifteen feet, a third deep, and the dust ran down it and gathered at the end. Then he took the read off it, deliberately, the way you take your hand off a dog you have told to stay, and sent the read to Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:187:Cael stepped into the lane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:189:It carried him down past the place where Brom's forearm was going to be and was not yet. Lira's burst came off the wrong foot at the wrong angle and met nothing; she had read him going one way and the lane had taken him a third faster than any feet could. He came out of the end of the lane past Brom's shoulder, behind both of them, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:193:It was not in the hip and it was not in the shoulder. It was behind the eyes, a heaviness, as if somebody had laid a warm hand over the inside of his skull. And everything else he carried went slow with it. He reached for the Wind, out of curiosity, the way you test a stair, and it came, but it came late: a beat late, the way a dog comes when it has decided to come in its own time. He counted. For ten counts after the lane, everything answered him a beat behind his asking. At eleven it was gone, and the hand had lifted off the inside of his head, and the Wind came when he called it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:199:He told her exactly: behind the eyes, ten counts of everything slow, gone at eleven. She wrote it on her hand under the shoulder and the hip, small, because she was running out of hand. Then she stopped writing and looked up at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:201:"You wrote it settled," she said. "The flag. You wrote nothing pulls."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:203:"Nothing does." He had been turning it over himself since the count ran out. "Pull was the wrong thing answering. The push when I called the Wind, the read when I wanted my feet. That's gone. This is the right thing answering late." He thought back to the second floor in the dark, and the long while he had stood waiting for a pull that never came before he called anything at all. "At dawn I stood still after the lane until I was sure. I must have stood through the whole ten without counting them. Tonight was the first time I reached for anything inside them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:205:Lira looked at her palm. "So the flag's down, and this is the bill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:207:"The flag's down. This is the bill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:209:She wrote *bill, not flag* under the ten counts, and underlined it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:219:He had it by heart. Every notice he had ever had he knew by heart, because each of them had found him somewhere with nothing else to look at, a yard, a stair, a shut room, and the one from Norhold on one knee on a stone floor, with a healer holding his hand. *Note: pressure-differential component; corridor-seeding component. Short range.* At the time it had read like a clerk's description of somebody else's coat. Tonight he thought he could put his hand on each line of it. The lane was the corridor; he seeded it, and it grew where he put it, and stayed while he left it alone. The tilt of the air, the ears, the dust running downhill, that was the pressure. Short range was fifteen feet. And the notice had said nothing at all about the read going into the air and leaving the body behind, or about ten counts of everything slow. Notices never told you what a thing cost. They only told you what it was. The cost you found out on a floor, with friends, if you were lucky, and in front of eight thousand people if you were not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:243:It was going down. It went down a little every month, and it had never stopped. Rent from waking, every day, spent or not. He had never used it, not once, not even in the last corner of the hardest fight of his life, when it had offered itself to him like a hand held out. It was sealed for a debt, and the debt was to a quiet man who sat at the middle of the bench and cut his bread into squares, and who did not know it was owed, or what for. Nobody had told him yet. The four of them had decided that at Norhold, on the night of the seal. Not yet. When it could be told whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:269:Lira held up her hand to the lantern and read it aloud, squinting at the pencil marks running across her palm and down onto her wrist. "Hip, crest to knee, the eighth into the thigh. Shoulder, five, the fifth counted. Forearm: four in a row, the fourth to the elbow; it's a cup, not a wall. Spark: one, on Brom, who said hm. The lane: fifteen feet, a third deep. First time, the read went into the air and Brom put him on the floor. Second time, lane first, read second, through. After: behind the eyes, ten counts slow, gone at eleven." She lowered the hand. "One drift. Held." She looked at her palm. "Karis is going to make me copy this out three times because of my capitals."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:273:"I'm telling her the bit about the floor first," said Lira. "Which thing, then writing." She sat up. "Bed. Somebody's hip is going to send its bill at the first bell, and I want to be awake to hear about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:279:He took the residence stair one step at a time on Seventh-day morning and went down to the north wall to let the sun get into it. It was a rest day. The house was mostly asleep, or pretending, and the Crown yard lay empty under the long wall, where the porter's boards stood chalked fresh with the year's names. Down in the valley the two coats stood at the foot of the road and the two at the landing, and none of them looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:283:The records hall stood across the court from the wall, its long roof and its row of tall windows facing east toward the valley. In the highest window, the one at the north end that lit the stair to Bracken's rooms, a man was standing. He had a small brass glass in his hand, the kind a ship's officer carries, and every so often he lifted it and looked through it down the valley, toward the place where the bluff road came up out of the trees. Then he lowered it, and stood, and lifted it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:285:Cael watched him do it four times. Then he got up, and took the long way round the court so as not to come at the records hall from the direction of the wall, and went in by the side door and up the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:289:"Good morning," said Bracken. "Your hip is bad. I heard you on the stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:11:He sat in the visitor's chair with the folder on his knees. He opened it at the back, because that was where the house's counsel would have started.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:19:He turned back a leaf, and another. The house's placings, a long thin column of meets and figures, and last year's the only one with *third* in it. Inspections, each with its date and its officer's name and its finding. Near the front he came to the only two leaves in the folder written in two different inks. On each, the left side was an inspector's complaint and the right side was the house answering it, in another year and another hand. He read the right-hand sides first, because he had just been reading one that was empty: a book kept again, and below the house's answer a visiting officer's clearance; an index finished, and below that answer another officer's clearance. Only then did he look across to see what they had answered. A quarter's lamp-hours nobody had written down. A year's declarations nobody had indexed. Both were long before Withrow. Then the charter's grant on its heavier paper, under a seal gone the colour of tea.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:23:"There's no form for taking a leaf out," said Withrow. "I looked, once, at the registry, for somebody else's house. There's a form for everything else." She did not move her hands. "Suppose the seat is with us on every point. A clerk comes up our road with a stamp, and writes *sustained* in that column, and I give him tea. That's the best day this folder can have. And the next inspector starts where they're all taught to start, at the grant, and turns forward, and the last thing he reads before he goes out to look at our floors is that this house once stood in front of a seat for what it did with you. If the seat isn't with us, the column says that instead, and he stays a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:35:"Karis's doors are real," she said. "I sat with her slips the night she laid them out, and I've walked every one of them since, the way you'd walk a building somebody had asked you to insure. They open one way. She's right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:53:"I did know it might," she said. "Not the hood. I'd never have guessed the hood. But a folder, yes, one day, and a column with nothing in it." She brought her eyes back to him. "That's what I meant when I told the hall to leave what it is where anybody can check. Somebody checks. Then you find out whether you meant it." She stood. "I meant it. I'd like that to be the last time either of us asks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:63:Every other mark on the page was a date somebody he knew had written down. That one would be written by a hand under a hood, on a morning he could not yet see. He left a gap on the line for it, and found he had drawn the gap carefully, the way you leave a space at a table for somebody who has not yet come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:71:"A street and a half apart." Karis looked at the slip. "Lira will be sure somebody arranged it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:111:She could not drill. She went every dawn regardless and sat at the rail with Rooke's red drill-book and watched the program work under the two lamps, because she said a drill watched was half a drill done, and that she would rather be half than nothing. This morning she did not go to the rail by the wall. She stood a moment in the doorway with her hand on the frame. Then she let go and walked across the painted floor between the white crosses and arrows to where Rooke stood at the far rail with his arms folded, in front of the whole program, at her own pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:131:He came into the reading room with a flat packet under his arm wrapped in oilcloth and sealed with the Greyvane archive's grey wax, and stood in the doorway in his spectacles and weighed it on his palm out of habit before he held it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:229:She had the bench by the rail, with Rooke's red drill-book on her knee for something to hold and her right foot out straight on the boards. Karis had chalk and her grey notebook and her coat buttoned right for once. Brom had gone up the gallery stair and drawn every shutter down the length of the north wall, as Lira had drawn them on the first honest floor of the year. Now he stood in the hall's great doorway, with one of the two tall doors standing open a hand's breadth onto the dark of the passage and his right hand on its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:271:Brom moved his weight in the doorway. Cael felt it before he heard it: a rest lifting, and another place on the boards, half a pace to the left, where a foot was about to come down, lit faintly a breath before the foot came. He opened his eyes in time to see Brom's boot come down there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:279:He had stood in the doorway all this while with his right hand on the edge of the open door, because somebody had to be in the door, and he had noticed something that Cael had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:311:Cael stood in the middle of the painted floor and looked at Brom's hand on the edge of the door, the big square hand that had held twelve feet of road against two men on the Ostrand road and held a girl up in a doorway for twelve seconds. Something in him went quiet that had nothing to do with the new thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:331:It asked as it asked about a door. Not about the flames themselves, which moved and were not at rest; about the line between them. Two points that stayed where they were put, and a line drawn from one to the other, white on the boards: to the new thing that was a threshold. It leaned toward the chalk line between the two small fires exactly as it leaned toward an open door, with its whole courteous attention. *There?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:333:"It wants the line," said Cael. "It thinks it's a doorway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:335:"Two points and a line between them," said Karis. "That's all a doorway is, to it, isn't it? Jamb and jamb and the space between." She wrote it. "Name it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:341:She put the two flames out with a closing of her hand, and stood, and moved the line. She drew it again two feet further from him, and lit its ends again, and counted three and four, and the new thing asked, and he named it. She moved it two feet further and counted five and six, and it asked a little less surely, as a voice is less sure across a wider room. At ten feet he named it, and Karis wrote *10* in her notebook and drew a ring round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:343:At twelve feet she lit the seventh and the eighth, and the asking was very faint, like somebody speaking in the next room with the door shut. At fourteen she drew the line and did not light it, and asked him, and there was nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:351:She went back to the line at twelve feet, to put out the last two flames, and it happened then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:353:The chalk line at twelve feet lay on the boards between its two small fires, and the new thing, which had been faint about it, was suddenly not faint. It leaned. It leaned the way it had leaned in his own doorway with Lira coming through it on her crutch, when he had been halfway through saying *no*. Something in him began to go out of him toward the line, quick and quiet as a breath, to set a point at one end of it and a point at the other and tell the space between them to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:129:He did not sit down to say it, and he did not raise his voice. He stood at the middle of the table's far side with the whole of the case laid out in front of him in the lamplight, the forty and the bundle and the two stacks and the leaf, and he said it to the room the way he had once read a form to a man in a doorway at the third hour, one plain sentence after another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:171:It was a deep straight cutting through a spur of the hill that came down to the river. Somebody a long time ago had decided to go through the spur rather than round it, and had dug a road down into the chalk and carted the chalk away, and the walls were still there, white and steep and streaked with green where the water ran. On the north side, uphill, the bank stood twenty feet over the road. On the south side, toward the river, it was lower, ten or twelve feet. Toward the far end of the cut, a stone's throw short of where it opened out again, the south bank had been eaten away underneath by some flood long ago, before the road had a ditch, so that its lip hung out over a hollow of shadow, like a brow over an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:205:Together they made a frame. He saw its shape as he knelt there, as clearly as he had seen the chalk court on the oak. Both mouths of the cut were closed. The west mouth behind them had three points across it, and the east had the cart and the culvert and the milestone. The north bank was too high. The middle of the road was held. There was one way through the whole cut that the points did not close, and it was a channel along the foot of the south bank, under the overhanging lip, perhaps twelve feet wide, running east past the cart's tail to the culvert.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:233:He looked at the hobbles and the folded hood and the soft rope, and the staves that had been chosen because they bruised and did not cut, and the six faces that were not faces. He looked at the channel along the south bank, twelve feet wide, running to the culvert. He thought of the gully beyond it, which he could not see into, and what a man would keep there if he had come to take a person somewhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:At Norhold the wrong thing had answered. Here nothing answered at all. He knelt in the dust and called his own legs, out of habit, to get him up, and the calling went out of him and found nobody at home. The Wind did not lift its head. The read lay where he had left it. Everything he carried had turned, all together and very politely, to look at the new thing standing in the doorway, and none of them was listening to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:29:Note: fixed-point binding component; lattice-perception component.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:38:*Silver*, it said. It was the first notice that had ever said Silver. The guess on the leaf had been right; he had priced it at the weight it came at. *Anchor-adjacent*, it said, and he thought of Seln at the long table with his eyes shut, saying the name, and Karis counting the words off on her fingers, keeping them straight. *Fixed-point binding.* That was the thing against his coat, and the thing he had let go of. *Lattice-perception.* That was the grey net he had seen laid down the whole cut, which he could not see now at all. *Contact-to-short range.* Close to her, cheap. Further off, at a price he had watched her pay, half a second at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:268:He did not feel anything break. He had expected, if it ever came, that it would break like a bone, with a noise. It went like the cage had gone on the road: one stitch, and then all the rest after it, quietly, in the order they had been laid. Every receipt he had ever counted was still true. Every one of them was still in the book. They simply did not hold anything up any more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:286:The house's healer was a spare grey woman who had strapped every fighter on the hill for twenty years and had long ago stopped asking how anybody had come by anything. She looked at the five of them in her doorway, in their chalk, and sent Seln away at once, because there was nothing wrong with him but wet knees, and set the other four out along her benches in the order she meant to deal with them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:87:He stood a moment in the shop doorway with the twine in his hand, out of the rain, and watched the street. A dray going over the bridge with its load sheeted. Two boys with a dog. A woman under a shawl buying eels from a barrow. The bridge-ward in his oilskin at the near end of the parapet, looking down at the water against the old piers and then at the iron rings let into the stone of the middle one, the way a man looks at a clock he expects to be late. There were five rings on that pier, one above the other. The river was at the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:121:He told it the way he had written it, standing against a wall under a lamp in the covered walk with the notebook flat on the stone at the third hour. The archive at Halcenvane on a bad night two years ago, near enough, in the first year he was on the hill. The fire-watch gone the long way round. A man with a bag at the top of the upper flight, in the doorway, with his gloved hand already going into a press he had no business opening. And on the landing above him, in the light, the wing's assistant. He was reading the man a form, one plain line after another, about copying and paper and what a magistrate counted in years, until the man's hand came back out of the press with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:129:"I knew there was somebody in the corner on the second night, and I knew who on the third." Seln's voice had not changed at all. "I did not look down the flight. A man in a doorway with a bag looks where the officer looks, so I did not look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:156:"A year of it," said Seln. "Before I was a man in a doorway, I glowed." He did not explain that, and Brom, in the chair by the shelf, looked at the floor as if he had heard it before somewhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:99:The doorway was in the middle of the afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:107:*There?* said the new thing, about the doorway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:113:He felt it go. It went out of him as quick and quiet as a breath, without his leave, without his asking, without his having said anything at all but the first sound of the word *no*. It set a point against the left-hand jamb of the door, at the height of a woman's shoulder, and another against the right-hand jamb, and drew the line between them taut across the opening, and told it to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:117:She walked into air. He saw her do it from the bed, with his eyes open now and his whole body cold. She came forward on her good foot with the crutch swinging ahead of her to take the next step, and the crutch's tip went through the doorway, and her shoulder did not. Her shoulder met the line across the doorway as a shoulder meets a door that somebody has shut in your face, and she stopped dead, and the crutch went on without her. Its rubber foot skidded on the boards, and her weight went after it, onto the strapped ankle, and she made a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:125:Cael was off the bed without any memory of getting off it; his ribs would tell him about it a long while afterward. He was standing in the middle of the room looking at a doorway with nothing in it, and Lira's face on the far side of the nothing, white, with her shoulder still pressed against the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:133:Lira had stopped pushing against it. She stood in Brom's arm and put her open hand flat on the nothing in the doorway, at the height of her shoulder, and held it there, and looked at Cael through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:137:He did not try again, because he did not know what else to try. He stood in the middle of his room with his hands open and looked at her, and she looked at him, and the doorway held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:145:It let go by itself, without his asking. He felt the point leave the left jamb and the right, and the line between them go slack and simply not be there, and Lira's flat hand went forward through the doorway into the room as if somebody had opened a door she had been leaning on. Brom held her up. Her crutch lay on the boards inside the room where it had skidded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:169:"So does your Wind," said Lira, "and nobody blames you when it knocks a tray off a table on the oak." She put her foot more squarely on the stool. "Write that it was in you and it wasn't you. Both. They're both true, and I was the one in the doorway, so I get to say which goes first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:171:He shut the door, carefully, all the way, until the latch clicked. Then he sat down on the end of the bed, because Lira had the chair, and nobody said anything else until Karis came at the fifth bell with her squared paper and stopped in the doorway, which was only a doorway, and looked at the three of them, and asked what had happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:179:He stood in the doorway with his hands in his coat pockets and did not come in. He looked at Lira's foot on the stool and at Brom's sling and at the strapping round Cael's ribs where his shirt was open, and at the rota nailed to the inside of the door. He read the rota. Cael watched him read it, all of it, down to *SOMEBODY IN THE ROOM. ALWAYS.*, and then look at the doorway he was standing in, as if it had told him something he did not have words for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:211:"The leaf. I sat at the sill with all of you on the Second-day and priced it. I wrote down everything I could think of, the first minute and a week without the band and the lane and the read and somebody in the room. I left blanks where I didn't know, the way Seln said." He looked at the door. "There wasn't a line for that. There wasn't a line that said it might do something on its own, in a doorway, to Lira, with me lying here saying *no*. I didn't think of it. I measured it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:231:"You sat at that sill on the Second-day with the rain on the window and measured it cold," said Brom. "Three times. With all of us. You wrote down what you could see and left blanks where you couldn't. Then it went in the fire, on a road, and it did something in a doorway that nobody could have measured, because nobody's ever had this iron before." He opened his eyes at last and looked at Cael across the dark room. "You've measured. That's the part that's yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:235:"And the doorway?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:237:"The doorway's the fire's," said Brom. "We'll write it down. Next time there's a leaf, it'll have a line for doorways." He shut his eyes again. "Go to sleep. I'm counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:286:The marks went down the sheet in their rows: forty in the first hour she had counted, then thirty-eight, thirty-six, thirty-one after the storm moved, thirty, twenty-eight. A line at the doorway with a box round it. Twenty-two through the night, by Brom's count and Lira's, which they had kept on the backs of their hands. Then the dawn, and the naming, and the counts dropped off the bottom of the page all at once: nine, four, two, one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:308:He did not come in, but stood in the doorway, which was only a doorway, and looked at the rota on the door and the squared paper on Karis's knee and did not ask about either. He held out the letter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:322:*Not on the leaf: the doorway. Twelve seconds, uncommanded, with Lira in it. Brom says that's the fire's and not the rule's. I'm writing it down so the next leaf has a line for doorways.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:81:He put his right palm flat against the point at his breastbone, where his chest had found it, and leaned, and then did not lean. He gave it the push at fighting load, the thing he had taken from the Bronze at Ardenmere, the thing he had given a Gold five times in one sitting at Norhold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:157:Eight feet on there was a point at the height of his ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:191:There was a cage round him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:199:He had been carrying it since the first exchange. He understood that now, kneeling. When his chest had found the point at his breastbone she had not only stopped him. She had laid a second point against him, against the cloth over Hesk's book, so lightly that he had taken it for the bruise of hitting the first one. It went where he went. It had gone with him on every push and every turn and down the camber. It was the first point of the cage, and every other point in the cage was laid from it, and to it, and through it, the way every stitch in a row of knitting is worked through the stitch before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:203:Three more, and it would close. He could feel exactly where. Three gaps, one at his left shoulder, one at his right hip, one over his head. When those three were laid, the cage would stop being a cage and start being a hold. A person inside a closed hold did not walk to a culvert. He was carried there, quite gently, by people who did not need to touch him at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:He knelt in the cage and held still, all of him, in front of four exchanges of her. That was all it had ever been. Not taking anything. He turned everything he had left toward what he had watched her do, and kept it there, open. Subtraction. The points laid where his half beat said he would go. The stillness of a woman threading a needle at the edge of her reach, half a second's price for range. The accumulation that never gave anything back. The stitch against his coat, and everything worked through it. He had paid for every one of those in his own body on this road, and he knew them now as well as he knew the stair to his own room with the lamps out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:225:Every course in the cut stood out to him as plainly as the chalk dust had stood out along his lane: taut grey threads in the air, from point to point to point, a great slack net laid down the whole length of the road, closed at both ends, open in one channel where Brom stood. He saw the nineteen round him, each with its line to the next. He saw the one against his coat, the first stitch, with every other line in the cage worked through it. And he saw how it was held. It was held from inside, from where it touched him. It was the same thing as the point at his breastbone and the one under the horse's leg and the ones that held the coachman's hands. It was a thing set against a surface and told to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:231:It went the way you let go of a breath you have been holding without knowing it. The point against his coat was simply not there any more. And because every other point in the cage had been laid through it, they went after it, one after another, in the order she had laid them, unthreading round him in a quick soft ripple like a row of stitches coming out when the first is pulled. The nineteen went. Their lines went out to the fourteen in the cut, and the fourteen went too. He felt them go down the road behind him like a rope paying out over a wheel: the three across the west mouth, the two in the south bank's lip, the three high in the north bank, the parapets, the milestone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:3:Rooke struck the three bouts before breakfast, standing at the program's board in the east hall's doorway with a stick of chalk, and Cael watched him do it from the foot of the residence stair because his ribs had made the stair a thing to be taken slowly and he had stopped halfway to rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:33:He had been seventeen for a year. It had been a long one. He had been seventeen at Norhold on the stone with a healer's fingers in his hand, and seventeen on the road home in a cart, and seventeen behind a bell-frame on a gatehouse roof, and seventeen five days ago on his knees in a chalk cut with a cage of points round him. And now he was not. Nothing in him had changed in the night. Nothing ever did, on a birthday. It was only a line in somebody's register moving down a row, but it was his line, and it had moved, and Lira had said it out loud across a refectory table before anybody else could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:41:Bracken brought it up himself. He did not have to; the porter's shoulder was mending and Seln was doing the sort, and Seln would have put it in Cael's hole with the rest. But Bracken came up the residence stair with it under his arm at the noon bell, and knocked, and held it out in the doorway, which was only a doorway, and did not come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:77:He stopped in the doorway and stood there with one hand on the frame, a tall loose young man in the cohort's coat with the green-taped captain's file under his arm, because he went nowhere without it. He looked at the four of them. Then he looked at the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:99:He came up the library stair without a sound, as he came up every stair, and stopped in the doorway, and did not come in at once. He stood there with his hands at his sides and looked at the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:199:At the reading room's door he had felt it ask. He had felt it on every doorway since the road, and this one was a good door, oak, heavy, set square in its frame, the kind of door the new thing wanted most. *There?* He had named it under his breath. *Door. Karis's. It opens.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:229:*Wind-adjacent — Lira, the burst. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere, the push. Iron-adjacent — Brom, the read, which found the fourteen. Compression-adjacent — Reydan, the give. Ember-adjacent — Karis, the spark, two contacts and no more. Shadow-adjacent — the stair, sealed, rent from waking. Storm-adjacent — Daeva, the lane; moved twice this week, a quarter hour and then forty minutes, and settled. Anchor-adjacent — the hand with no face; idle by naming; wants doorways.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:157:Seln stood on the landing with his coat buttoned to the throat and something square under it, and he did not come in. He stood in the doorway and looked at the room in the order Cael knew. The door, the lock, the card; the two windows, the lit one with its lamp, the narrow one at the end, dark, with the library's roof below it; the landing behind him, the annex door, the stair; the bare table and the shaded lamp; then the four of them, one at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:141:Cael stood in the doorway and did not say anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:251:That was all. He did not seem to think it needed more, and after a moment Cael understood that it did not. Brom had held twelve feet of road with one arm, and had sat a rota outside a door for two nights without being asked. Of course it was Brom who counted, and counted again when the first count was right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:He had the line laid already. He did not cut at her; he simply turned his wrist, and the lath came up and lay along the path she was travelling, like a bar dropped across a doorway, and she arrived on it. It took her on the forearm, the left, the one she carried close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:117:Cael stood in the doorway and looked at it in Seln's order before he knew he was doing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:225:He gave her the whole of his attention before he answered. It was the look he had given the room from the doorway, slow and level and entire, the look of a man who has been sent to evaluate something and means to do it fairly even when the something is a girl with a turned ankle asking him a straight question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:279:He thought of the room in Ostrand, though he had told himself on the post road that he would not. Four young people and a man at a window. The man had stood a little to one side of the glass, where the frame hid him from the lane, the way the field service teaches its people to stand, and had copied forty orders in a round level hand that never once let itself be hurried. The Archmarshal had known what he was from the doorway. He had known, reading, roughly what the copying must have cost and how many years it had sat somewhere before it came to that table. He did not know the man's name, and he had been careful all the way home not to wonder it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:299:He had a file under his arm, a different one, and a question about a guild hall's frame that took a minute to ask and a minute to answer. When it was answered he did not go. He stood in the doorway with the file against his chest, nervous and good, and looked past the Archmarshal at the tray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:319:At the sixth bell his clerk stood in the doorway with the day's wallet against his chest, looking not at the desk but past it, at the window, where the evening had come in grey.

