   125	"That was the old way," she said. "Before the policy. An assessor who met a reading that went across many families could write that, pending further study, and the person went on with his life under that word, and nobody corrected anything. It was a door the old system left open. And the first three memoranda shut it, in order. Each of them cites an article of the directive by its number, the same directive I sat over at Halcenvane." She counted on her fingers, slowly, one, two, three. "The first: *close the door.* No assessor may designate by judgment of what a person shows; a station enters what the reading gives, and only that. The second: *remove the exit.* The provisional custom, pending further study, is struck; there is to be no designation that waits on study. The third: *delete the name.* The old word is discontinued, and every person who carried it is to be re-entered under a flag."
   126	
   127	She did not say which flag, and she did not have to. Cael's own schedule two had it, bracketed, under *Path*, on the instrument on his sill.
   128	
   129	"That's the word your Arbiter said at Denvash," said Karis, quite gently. "It isn't a classification, Cael, and it never was. It's what they wrote where the old name used to be."
   130	
   131	He found that he had known it for a long time, in the way you know a thing you have never let yourself look at. He sat with it. It did not hurt the way he had thought it would. It was more like setting down something he had been carrying the wrong way round for years and finding that it was lighter the right way up.
   132	
   133	Karis turned the second slip over.
   134	
   135	"And that's where the case ends," she said. "So here is the limit."
   136	
   137	She did not read it from the slip. She said it to the room, slowly, in the voice she kept for the last line of a finding, the line she would be held to.
   138	
   139	"Here is the limit, and I am entering it as a limit and not a finding, because the difference is the whole of my discipline."
   140	

