editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:213:It did not ask, for the quiet thing from the stair never asked. It only offered, as it had offered every day of his life for two years, and it offered now with a kind of patience, as if it had been waiting since supper for him to see the sense of it. A man was sitting somewhere ahead in the dark, counting, and Cael was trying not to be counted; and here in him, sealed and paid for every morning since he woke, was the one thing he owned that could take him down that track past any man alive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:93:"The hall keeps no recess," said Bracken, before he could ask. "Enrollees go home. Paper does not." He turned the daybook round on the counter so that Cael could read the morning's entry, which was a thing he did only when he was pleased with it. *Recess, the first day. In residence: forty-one enrolled, by slip. Post: the small bag. Nothing sealed.* "Nothing sealed," he repeated, and turned it back. "I have written that at the foot of the day for a week running, and I find I look forward to it. Go and be idle somewhere. It is what a recess is for, and I am told it does not come naturally to everybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:139:[unnamed] — Shadow-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:160:"The night before the final," said Cael. "In a back room on the harbour, with the shutter open a crack. We'd costed everything I was going to spend the next day: the Wind past six, the push at fighting load, the give, the spark. Everything that had been held back for two years was going to go out in one afternoon, in front of the whole continent, and be on the record for good." He looked at Lira. "And I said the quiet thing stayed sealed, and Lira said to cost the seal, out loud, and say why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:168:"So it stayed sealed. It was sealed that night for a debt, and the next afternoon, in the last corner, against the barrier, with her lane coming and nowhere left to go, it offered. It always offers when I'm cornered, and it would have hidden me from every eye in the bowl." He let his breath go. "I didn't turn my head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:188:"Is it still sealed?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:194:"It's sealed till somebody unseals it," said Cael. "I don't know who that is now. It was a debt, and you've been told. I don't know what a debt is after it's been told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:198:"I am not going to release it," he said, "and I am not going to take it back. I couldn't if I wanted to. It is yours. A notice says so, and I have never yet known one of those to be wrong either." He was quiet a moment. "But I would like to set the terms of the seal. If you will let me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:202:"While its use can be seen by anyone who could carry the seeing west, it stays where it is." Seln said it slowly and exactly, the way he wrote a line in a return that he meant to be read by somebody careful. "Not for my file, which can look after itself now; it has had practice. For the trade. Somewhere, in a room I will never see, there is a man whose whole work is sorting, and any report of a thing that moves the way I move gives him a thread to pull. Where there is no witness who can carry it west, there is no thread, and then it is yours. Use it or don't, as you judge." He unfolded his hands. "And you will not ask me first. That is the other term."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:208:*Shadow-adjacent, the quiet thing from the stair. Told tonight, whole, to the man it came from, in Karis's room, the four of us there. The debt is closed. The seal is kept. Grounds revised: no longer a debt kept from him. His terms: sealed from any witness who could carry it west; otherwise mine, and I don't ask.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:266:Then he turned back three leaves to the birthday inventory, to the line for the sixth, and drew one straight line through *sealed, rent from waking* so that it could still be read, the way Seln's mother had done in a grain factor's ledger on a river quay, and wrote the new figure beside it in the margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:268:*Sealed, on his terms. Rent from waking.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:272:Through the window, down in the court, a man in a dark coat came out of the library's shadow at the bindery end and crossed the flags toward the wing. He went at an even pace, neither slow nor quick. He did not take the covered walk. He went straight across the open court in the frost where anybody could have seen him, and nobody did, because nobody looks at a man going home at the tenth bell with nothing in his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:257:A single leaf of registry stock, under the seat's seal at Ostrand, the river and the scale. He knew the form. He had answered it, in one shape or another, perhaps two hundred times in forty years, and it had never once before taken him more than the time it takes to dip a pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:263:He read the leaf through, as he read everything, from its head to its seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:297:He drew the next thing toward him from the tray on the right, a guild hall's query about a frame. He read it through from its head to its seal, and dipped his pen, and answered it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:163:She had started it in the dairy week. Not that night; the night after, when she could not sleep for thinking about three carters and a road with four days of stamps on it and then none. She had thought then, lying on her back in the dark, about what she actually owned. Not the program. The program was Rooke's, and the east hall was the house's, and the red book belonged to a river academy and was only lent. The figure on the steward's sheet was hers, but it was hers the way a room is yours when somebody else holds the key; it meant something because a hall's seal was on it, and it would go on meaning something exactly as far as that seal was honoured.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:167:So she had started writing down what she knew. Not what she had won. What she knew, in her own words, in pencil, in a carter's book, so that it would still be hers out where no hall's seal went with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:77:Not all of it. The wing's other sealed things still stood there in their row, a box of old sigil cards and two bound runs of the wing's returns and a tin of sealing wafers. But at the end of the row, where the case had stood for two years, there was only a clean oblong of wood, a shade paler than the wood round it, with a soft grey edge of dust on three sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. Schedule of evidence. The panel will read, first, the practitioner's record as entered, the continental title included. Second, the house's attendance and floor sheets for the two years preceding, to be supplied by the registrar under the house's seal. Third, a reading in the sigil frame, under load, taken on the day in the presence of the three assessors. No testimony is contemplated. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the second bell.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:255:Fifteen feet of it, from his chalk to the space behind her left shoulder, a third deep. His ears closed and opened. The dust in the cracks of the boards woke and ran, pale, down the lane, and under the lanterns it threw a thin moving shadow, and he saw her see the shadow and know it. She had learned to read that band on this floor in the year's seventh week. She burst along its edge to get out from under where he would arrive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:87:"And then a note." Seln took a small square of plain paper from his waistcoat pocket, the kind the wing used for marking places in ledgers, and laid it on the cloth, already written. "No name, no seal, no hand anybody would know. An address and an hour, and one line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:171:He read the memoranda from the bottom, as Karis had, without being told. He read the seals. At the third issue he took out a small lens on a cord from inside his coat and looked at the seal through it, and at the seventh, and at the nineteenth, and put the lens away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:211:"The second is a report. I have sworn to every report I ever made, and the oath is made to the office that receives it." He looked at the memoranda, at the nineteen seals. "That office is the one that wrote these. I should be swearing, in my best hand, to the man who forged them, and he would read it with great courtesy and enter it and thank me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:283:Then, because it was what he was, he went back through it as he would have made a witness go back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:291:He could call the junior up the too-steep stair and tell him. He thought of the young man's face, nervous and good. He would make him a witness to a thing he could never afterward unknow, and send him back to a career of writing *within tolerance* under margins he would know had been cleaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:13:"The house's copy," said Seln. "Thank you." He unfolded it on the counter and read it, and Cael watched him read it. It was a station certificate, the ordinary kind, with a registry seal at its foot and three lines of a clerk's writing above. Seln read the three lines once at his counter pace, neither slow nor quick. Then he blotted the foot where the girl's mother had signed for it, though the ink was days dry, and folded it again along its old folds, and wrote a line in the wing's ledger, and put the certificate in the drawer under the counter where enrollees' station papers went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:131:He came into the reading room with a flat packet under his arm wrapped in oilcloth and sealed with the Greyvane archive's grey wax, and stood in the doorway in his spectacles and weighed it on his palm out of habit before he held it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:133:"Nine days on the road," he said. "Registered, under seal, with the archive's compliments and an invoice for the copying that I have paid and entered. The first spend on the house's first budget line for an enrollee's research." He gave it to her. "It came before the recess, as they promised. I note it because the archive at Greyvane has never in my thirteen years kept a promise about a date, and I wish it to be on the record that it has kept one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:135:Karis signed his receipt with her right hand, the pale fingers steady on the pen. Then she broke the seal and folded back the oilcloth and looked at what was inside for a long moment without touching it, the way she had looked at the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:137:Nine sheets, each with a seal impression taken in fresh wax from an old die and a line of signatures copied under it in an archivist's careful hand. The founders' hands, and the registry seals that had been set beside them on the charter, one for every year the charter had been renewed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:141:*Received, the house's ninety-first day. Under seal, entire.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:143:*Nine leaves. Seals of known years, one to a renewal.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:7:Cael went up on the third night only to bring her bread, because Brom said somebody had to and Brom's shoulder did not like the top stair yet. She did not look up when he came in. The forty pairs were not in four rows any longer. They lay in a single long line down the whole of the table, sorted by something he could not see, with a slip of paper on each in her small upright hand. The memoranda stood beside them in nineteen separate piles, one issue to a pile. Every seal on every one of them had a numbered slip tucked under it, and the nine new leaves from Greyvane lay open across the window end in their fresh grey wax. Seln sat at the middle of the bench with his hands flat and watched her. Karis asked Cael one question without looking up, about Denvash, and he answered it standing at her elbow, and Seln said nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:101:"Nine renewals of Greyvane's charter, nine registry seals set beside the founders' hands, nine years written under them in the charter's own text. I've never in my life had nine seals with years on them side by side before. I laid every memorandum's seal against them, under the glass, ring by ring." She looked at Seln. "The newest issue is from this generation. The seventh falls between Greyvane's fourth renewal and its fifth. The oldest are older than any seal Greyvane has. I counted back from where they meet, ten years an issue, near enough, the way the seals change." She put the slip down. "The first memorandum is two hundred years old, give or take a decade."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:37:So she started at the oldest, at the yellow end, and worked forward. The sheets were printed, all of them, each a single leaf in a registry circular's form, with a number at the head and a seal pressed at the foot and a line of signatures under the seal. Cael watched her turn them, and the paper changed as she went, from a thick soft yellow stock that had gone brown at its edges to a thinner whiter one, and then to the hard bright paper the registry used now, the paper of the instrument on his own sill. The type changed too. The oldest leaves had heavy letters with long tails, the kind on the founders' volumes Karis kept on her top shelf, and the newest had the narrow even type of every form Bracken had ever put across a counter. The seal at the foot of each changed by small degrees, a ring of letters gone and another come, the river and the scale drawn first one way and then another. The signatures changed with every issue, officer after officer, a new name every few leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:47:"Nineteen issues, reissued every ten years or so, by the look of the seals, when the officers changed or the paper did." Karis squared the stack. "Nineteen times somebody sat at a desk and had it set in type again, and signed it, and sent it to every station in the Compact."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:63:"Yes," said Karis. "The first one is the one I want." She drew it back into its place beside the others, very gently. "Not tonight. I want it in order first, all of it, every seal against every other seal. I won't guess at the first one; I'll find it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:He could not help it. Under every seal there was a line of them, three or four to an issue, and he went down them the way he went down the names on a bout card, by habit, and without meaning to he had counted. Nineteen issues and seventy-one names, give or take two he could not read in the yellow ones where the ink had gone brown and spread. Officers of the registry, by the titles after them: a chief clerk of entry, a keeper of the rolls, a second secretary, men and women whose offices Bracken would have known and Cael had never heard of. Not one of them appeared on two issues: ten years at a desk, and then a new name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:91:Cael could see it from the bench. It was small, smaller than any of the memoranda, a single leaf of a thick grey-brown paper that had a faint grain in it like cloth. The writing on it was not printed. It was ink, in a hand Cael had never seen, upright and very old-fashioned and very clear, with a heading and a short paragraph under it. There was no seal. At the foot there was a mark that might once have been a seal before something wore it away, or might never have been anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:139:Lira's hands had come together in her lap, one inside the other, and stayed there. Karis had not taken her gloves off. She sat with them resting on the cloth either side of the leaf, as if she had forgotten they were on. Brom's good hand had gone, very slowly, to the left side of his coat, and lay flat over the inside pocket where the summons was, and the docket, and the letter from Velmere that he had carried sealed since the forty-fifth day. He did not take anything out. He only kept his hand there, the way a man puts his hand on a wall in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:205:"I need it for five nights," said Karis, "to index it. Every seal against every seal, every hand against every hand, the stations on a map, the dates in a column. I can't do it from memory and I won't do it from notes I take out of this room." She looked at Seln. "So it stays here, or it goes back to your shelf, and I come down to the wing every night with a lamp, and somebody writes the lamp down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:233:They all knew what he meant. They had known since a back room in Norhold on the night before the final, when Lira had told him to cost the seal out loud, and he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:5:Cael sat up straight. He had sat up straight in that coach five times now, with his back to the horses and his good coat buttoned, and it had become a thing his spine did by itself when the wheels came off the last switchback onto the cobbles. Karis sat opposite with the document wallet on her knees and a board across the wallet. Beside her Bracken held the house's seal in its box on his lap, with both hands, as another man might have held a hat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:57:"The respondent filed, in the week before the recess, a supplemental table of the renewals of this enrollment, with the day of each sitting and the day of each seal set side by side. I have read it. I read it twice, on a boat." He turned his head a little toward the house's table, toward Karis, and inclined it. "It is correct. The petitioner accepts it as filed, in every line, and adopts it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"It follows," said Jent, "that the second schedule to the petition is wrong in one particular. It suggests, at its ninth line, that the first renewal was sealed out of its order. It was not. It was sealed nine days after the panel sat, because an officer of an inspecting delegation held the registry's copy for his countersignature, as he was entitled to do, and the respondent's advocate has shown the seat the very leaf." He took one sheet from the folder after all, and held it out to the clerk. "The petitioner withdraws that line, and tenders the schedule again without it. I should not like the seat to rule upon a schedule with a mistake in it. Least of all one of mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:103:The rain had thinned to a mist, and the market street ran shining down toward the river with the carts standing in it. Withrow had gone ahead to her carriage and bowed to nobody, and Bracken was at the coach door with the seal. Jent came down the four steps behind the house's party with his one folder under his arm, and at the bottom he stopped beside Karis and waited until she had to look at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:181:The house's exhibits stood at a hundred and seventy-nine by the end of the seventh sitting. He had receipted every one, and in eleven weeks he had not found among them a leaf out of order, a seal smudged past reading, or a count that did not come to what its contents leaf said it came to. He had sat junior to a good many proceedings, and had never sat junior to a record like it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:193:A mark in a boy's file with no office behind it. That was the first, and the oldest, and the hand it was written in was younger than his hand was now. Three more lay between, each in its year. The fifth was a sentence on a senior desk at Norhold, read upside down, shutting a door the first had found open. The sixth was a seal that stood in no registry of seals.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:199:He had thought, this morning a week ago, with the dry pen resting under a fourteenth line, that there might be a seventh. He could admit that here. What he had hoped, for the space of perhaps a breath, was a simple thing: that a designation written out under seal before a seat would have to name its office.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:225:"Thirty-four," said Withrow. "You like counting, so I'll give you the number before you start on it yourself. Thirty-four letters from the heads of other chartered houses, since the preliminary. They have come in ones and twos. Bracken puts them on my desk in the morning with the seals uppermost, so that I can see whose they are before I decide whether to have breakfast."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:261:"I know you will. That's why I told you." She drew the next letter from the taller pile and turned it to the lamp, and he saw the seal on it, blue-grey, the lake house's. "Go to bed. You've a floor the day after tomorrow, and I'm told you're to look ordinary on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:103:At the coach, in the exchange's yard, Bracken stood with the seal in its box and waited until everybody was in before he spoke. He did not get in himself, but stood on the cobbles with one hand on the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:109:"The same day. I can see both doors from the top of the market street." Bracken got in, and shut the door, and settled the seal on his knees. "I mention it only because nobody else was going to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:133:Cael had not seen it out of that pocket since the day it came. It was square, of a heavy cream paper, a little rubbed at the corners now from eighteen weeks of being carried against a man's ribs. The seal on its flap was a dark red wax with a device pressed into it that he had never been close enough to read. The seal had not been broken; there was not so much as a crack across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:135:Brom held it for a moment in both hands, and then he laid it on the table, by itself, a hand's breadth from the stack and a hand's breadth from the edge, with the seal uppermost. He put one thick finger on each of its near corners and turned it a very little, until its side lay true with the table's. He looked at it, and moved it the width of a nail, and looked again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:157:On the table under the window the envelope lay square to the edge, with its red seal up, in the last of the afternoon's light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:249:*Eight, unchanged. The new one idle; it wants thresholds and takes names instead. The quiet one sealed, on his terms, paid for every morning. Under the eight, the still place, uncounted.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:253:*On Brom's table: one envelope, sealed, square to the edge. Four weeks now, less a few days.*

