193:It was not in the hip and it was not in the shoulder. It was behind the eyes, a heaviness, as if somebody had laid a warm hand over the inside of his skull. And everything else he carried went slow with it. He reached for the Wind, out of curiosity, the way you test a stair, and it came, but it came late: a beat late, the way a dog comes when it has decided to come in its own time. He counted. For ten counts after the lane, everything answered him a beat behind his asking. At eleven it was gone, and the hand had lifted off the inside of his head, and the Wind came when he called it.
199:He told her exactly: behind the eyes, ten counts of everything slow, gone at eleven. She wrote it on her hand under the shoulder and the hip, small, because she was running out of hand. Then she stopped writing and looked up at him.
209:She wrote *bill, not flag* under the ten counts, and underlined it.
219:He had it by heart. Every notice he had ever had he knew by heart, because each of them had found him somewhere with nothing else to look at, a yard, a stair, a shut room, and the one from Norhold on one knee on a stone floor, with a healer holding his hand. *Note: pressure-differential component; corridor-seeding component. Short range.* At the time it had read like a clerk's description of somebody else's coat. Tonight he thought he could put his hand on each line of it. The lane was the corridor; he seeded it, and it grew where he put it, and stayed while he left it alone. The tilt of the air, the ears, the dust running downhill, that was the pressure. Short range was fifteen feet. And the notice had said nothing at all about the read going into the air and leaving the body behind, or about ten counts of everything slow. Notices never told you what a thing cost. They only told you what it was. The cost you found out on a floor, with friends, if you were lucky, and in front of eight thousand people if you were not.
269:Lira held up her hand to the lantern and read it aloud, squinting at the pencil marks running across her palm and down onto her wrist. "Hip, crest to knee, the eighth into the thigh. Shoulder, five, the fifth counted. Forearm: four in a row, the fourth to the elbow; it's a cup, not a wall. Spark: one, on Brom, who said hm. The lane: fifteen feet, a third deep. First time, the read went into the air and Brom put him on the floor. Second time, lane first, read second, through. After: behind the eyes, ten counts slow, gone at eleven." She lowered the hand. "One drift. Held." She looked at her palm. "Karis is going to make me copy this out three times because of my capitals."
191:There was a cage round him.
199:He had been carrying it since the first exchange. He understood that now, kneeling. When his chest had found the point at his breastbone she had not only stopped him. She had laid a second point against him, against the cloth over Hesk's book, so lightly that he had taken it for the bruise of hitting the first one. It went where he went. It had gone with him on every push and every turn and down the camber. It was the first point of the cage, and every other point in the cage was laid from it, and to it, and through it, the way every stitch in a row of knitting is worked through the stitch before.
203:Three more, and it would close. He could feel exactly where. Three gaps, one at his left shoulder, one at his right hip, one over his head. When those three were laid, the cage would stop being a cage and start being a hold. A person inside a closed hold did not walk to a culvert. He was carried there, quite gently, by people who did not need to touch him at all.
217:He knelt in the cage and held still, all of him, in front of four exchanges of her. That was all it had ever been. Not taking anything. He turned everything he had left toward what he had watched her do, and kept it there, open. Subtraction. The points laid where his half beat said he would go. The stillness of a woman threading a needle at the edge of her reach, half a second's price for range. The accumulation that never gave anything back. The stitch against his coat, and everything worked through it. He had paid for every one of those in his own body on this road, and he knew them now as well as he knew the stair to his own room with the lamps out.
225:Every course in the cut stood out to him as plainly as the chalk dust had stood out along his lane: taut grey threads in the air, from point to point to point, a great slack net laid down the whole length of the road, closed at both ends, open in one channel where Brom stood. He saw the nineteen round him, each with its line to the next. He saw the one against his coat, the first stitch, with every other line in the cage worked through it. And he saw how it was held. It was held from inside, from where it touched him. It was the same thing as the point at his breastbone and the one under the horse's leg and the ones that held the coachman's hands. It was a thing set against a surface and told to stay.
231:It went the way you let go of a breath you have been holding without knowing it. The point against his coat was simply not there any more. And because every other point in the cage had been laid through it, they went after it, one after another, in the order she had laid them, unthreading round him in a quick soft ripple like a row of stitches coming out when the first is pulled. The nineteen went. Their lines went out to the fourteen in the cut, and the fourteen went too. He felt them go down the road behind him like a rope paying out over a wheel: the three across the west mouth, the two in the south bank's lip, the three high in the north bank, the parapets, the milestone.
43:"He measures everything," said Brom, when the captain had gone.
47:"So do I." Brom unfolded his arms. "I don't mind being measured by somebody who's going to be right."
239:The hall came to him as places where things were resting. The frames along the east wall stood on their feet on the boards, and each foot was a place, a small sure point where weight met wood and stayed. The lantern on its hook by the rail hung, and its hook in the beam was a point, and the point was asking. The bench Lira sat on had four legs, and the legs were four points in a square. The bar across the foot of the gallery stair was two points and a line. The great door had its hinges, three of them, all points, and along its edge, where it stood open against nothing, a long empty place that the new thing leaned toward with its whole attention, the way a dog leans toward a door with somebody behind it.
257:"You've got your right heel on the boards and almost nothing on it. Everything's on the left foot and the bench." He could feel it, the two points where her feet met the floor, one heavy and sure and one hardly there at all, a point that barely asked. "You told Rooke it was better."
311:Cael stood in the middle of the painted floor and looked at Brom's hand on the edge of the door, the big square hand that had held twelve feet of road against two men on the Ostrand road and held a girl up in a doorway for twelve seconds. Something in him went quiet that had nothing to do with the new thing.
317:Karis's part came last, because it cost her something and she wanted it measured.
341:She put the two flames out with a closing of her hand, and stood, and moved the line. She drew it again two feet further from him, and lit its ends again, and counted three and four, and the new thing asked, and he named it. She moved it two feet further and counted five and six, and it asked a little less surely, as a voice is less sure across a wider room. At ten feet he named it, and Karis wrote *10* in her notebook and drew a ring round it.
343:At twelve feet she lit the seventh and the eighth, and the asking was very faint, like somebody speaking in the next room with the door shut. At fourteen she drew the line and did not light it, and asked him, and there was nothing at all.
345:"About ten," she said. "Sure to ten, faint at twelve, nothing at fourteen." She wrote it, and under it, *For now.* "That's what it can see. That's not what it can reach. Nobody's measured that, and nobody's going to tonight."
351:She went back to the line at twelve feet, to put out the last two flames, and it happened then.
353:The chalk line at twelve feet lay on the boards between its two small fires, and the new thing, which had been faint about it, was suddenly not faint. It leaned. It leaned the way it had leaned in his own doorway with Lira coming through it on her crutch, when he had been halfway through saying *no*. Something in him began to go out of him toward the line, quick and quiet as a breath, to set a point at one end of it and a point at the other and tell the space between them to stay.
160:"The night before the final," said Cael. "In a back room on the harbour, with the shutter open a crack. We'd costed everything I was going to spend the next day: the Wind past six, the push at fighting load, the give, the spark. Everything that had been held back for two years was going to go out in one afternoon, in front of the whole continent, and be on the record for good." He looked at Lira. "And I said the quiet thing stayed sealed, and Lira said to cost the seal, out loud, and say why."
168:"So it stayed sealed. It was sealed that night for a debt, and the next afternoon, in the last corner, against the barrier, with her lane coming and nowhere left to go, it offered. It always offers when I'm cornered, and it would have hidden me from every eye in the bowl." He let his breath go. "I didn't turn my head."
188:"Is it still sealed?"
194:"It's sealed till somebody unseals it," said Cael. "I don't know who that is now. It was a debt, and you've been told. I don't know what a debt is after it's been told."
198:"I am not going to release it," he said, "and I am not going to take it back. I couldn't if I wanted to. It is yours. A notice says so, and I have never yet known one of those to be wrong either." He was quiet a moment. "But I would like to set the terms of the seal. If you will let me."
202:"While its use can be seen by anyone who could carry the seeing west, it stays where it is." Seln said it slowly and exactly, the way he wrote a line in a return that he meant to be read by somebody careful. "Not for my file, which can look after itself now; it has had practice. For the trade. Somewhere, in a room I will never see, there is a man whose whole work is sorting, and any report of a thing that moves the way I move gives him a thread to pull. Where there is no witness who can carry it west, there is no thread, and then it is yours. Use it or don't, as you judge." He unfolded his hands. "And you will not ask me first. That is the other term."
208:*Shadow-adjacent, the quiet thing from the stair. Told tonight, whole, to the man it came from, in Karis's room, the four of us there. The debt is closed. The seal is kept. Grounds revised: no longer a debt kept from him. His terms: sealed from any witness who could carry it west; otherwise mine, and I don't ask.*
266:Then he turned back three leaves to the birthday inventory, to the line for the sixth, and drew one straight line through *sealed, rent from waking* so that it could still be read, the way Seln's mother had done in a grain factor's ledger on a river quay, and wrote the new figure beside it in the margin.
99:"You may say it to me twice more in your life," he said.
103:"What you have just told me, all of it, in order. Twice more, if there is ever a need, and you will know if there is, but not a third time." He said it as he would have said a rule of the wing to a first-year who had asked about floor hours. "Every telling is a copy, and a thing told three times is a thing on its way to being known. I have kept the case twelve years by never once describing it to anybody, and you will keep this by telling it as seldom as you can bear to." He paused. "I am not ungrateful. I am only telling you the price, because nobody told me mine until I had paid it."
182:"You kept my file too, then."
254:He had written everything for years. Every notice word for word, every price. The book was where a thing went to be true. And Seln had sat across the table and asked where it was written, and when he had answered, Seln had said *Under the bed*. In a voice that cost nothing, he had said, *A thing told three times is a thing on its way to being known.* The case had been kept twelve years by a man who had never once described it to anybody. Cael did not think he would last twelve hours if he started writing it down.
book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:81:"No frame has ever read anything off me but what I put in front of it. The plates in the wing read what I give them, and I give them the band. The read at Brom's grade is a surface, and I can hold a surface. And my own Arbiter went out at Denvash after one word. It's never lit since. I've not stood in a station since."
book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:127:She did not say which flag, and she did not have to. Cael's own schedule two had it, bracketed, under *Path*, on the instrument on his sill.
book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:129:"That's the word your Arbiter said at Denvash," said Karis, quite gently. "It isn't a classification, Cael, and it never was. It's what they wrote where the old name used to be."

